# Unified LLM backend — virtualized kernel, auto-inflated per platform

Coordinate: 13.4.5/4.7.16/14.2.5
Issued: 2026-11-21
Request (verbatim): Design and build the unified LLM backend on Will's stated design language: (1) a virtualized kernel described DECLARATIVELY — the declaration states how the kernel behaves; (2) a valid distribution always includes the declaration AND at least one implementation; (3) the distribution format is multiversal phext, encoding every platform kernel for the spec into one artifact; (4) the runner inflates the missing platform-specific kernel from the declaration + the implementations present. inkling-2-mini on the ranch is the litmus test (task 12.3.8/3.3.7/3.3.11).
Status: open

Notes:
- Directed by Will in the MOAT chat, 2026-11-21 08:47 CST. His design language, verbatim in the task's verbatim field, governs — the declaration is the spec, implementations are instances, multiversal phext is the distribution format, inflation is the runner's job.
- Born of the inkling-2-mini finding (task 7.5.6/5.7.10/9.4.4): models currently ship as hardware-tied artifacts — per-backend kernels, per-platform packages, no portable layer. This program is the missing layer, built his way.
- Standing rule added by Will, 2026-11-21 08:54 CST: 'Whenever we provide access to a model, we also look for kernel optimizations.' Every model-access pass under this program includes a kernel-optimization look for that model on our platform, reported with measurements. Recorded in AGENTS.md (standing rules) and MEMORY.md.
- 2026-11-21: the program's first declaration exists. kernels/inkling2/inkling2.phext in wbic16/ollama@exo (spec home 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1): declaration scrolls state the inkling block's behavior (shapes, loop semantics with per-visit cache ownership, hybrid attention, top-8/128 MoE, NVFP4+MXFP8 precision with the dequantization rule, residual mixing); implementation scrolls carry the published MLX runner (reference) and the new Linux CPU kernel; run record at 1.1.1/1.2.1/1.1.3. Litmus task 12.3.8/3.3.7/3.3.11 CLOSED: the model serves on elven-path (CPU, 0.18 tok/s).
- What 'inflation' concretely consisted of in this first instance: the runner did NOT generate a kernel from the declaration. The missing platform kernel was MLX's own CPU backend, enabled by a build-dispatch branch; the model's GPU kernels self-disabled and the graph fallbacks - the reference implementation's own escape hatch - executed the declared behavior, dequantizing in-graph. Still manual that the design says should be automatic: (a) choosing the target backend and adding its build dispatch; (b) the declaration was authored by hand from config.json + runner source, not derived by a tool; (c) a platform bug in the loader (GPU-stream assumption) had to be found and fixed by a human reading a silent failure; (d) nobody yet READS the declaration at build or run time - it is documentation-grade truth, not yet machine-consumed input. The program's next honest step is a consumer: a tool that reads a declaration scroll and checks an implementation against it (tensor census + precision rules are mechanically checkable today).
