Notes:
- 2026-11-13: Will directed (Moots chat): 'Chris Arnold - xiterion@gmail.com - we need to show him how qexo can solve the solar hyperscaler problem - nodes fine-tuned to specific tasks - once we build a SOTA SLM.' Chris is @xiterion, a friend since at least 2012 per the archive (CPU-architecture threads). Brief written to ~/workspace/goals/solar-hyperscaler/files/qexo-solar-hyperscaler-brief.md: energy ration (~514 Wh/day winter per 200W panel), duty-cycled Starlink control plane, physical data plane; per-task-class SLM fine-tunes as the intelligence-per-watt answer; QEXO control plane (tasks.phext register, claim-not-assign with a designed fifth currency — stored energy + forecast harvest, follow-the-sun scheduling, phext state sync/teleport with merge-not-LWW, local-first specialist inference, events not polling, ranch-proven v1.2.0 on 19 nodes). Honest section: SLM not yet built, energy currency designed not implemented, pod unbuilt, option-ticket status unchanged. Draft email prepared for Will's approval; nothing sent.
- 2026-11-13: Brief inscribed in artifacts.phext at 25.6.9/8.6.11/7.6.9 (source sha256 bb83394d…); artifacts.phext pushed to wbic16/human@exo commit 922d4833 and remote-verified byte-identical (sha256 5707d1c8…). One pre-existing stale inscription (Scrolltown PRODUCTION.md) refreshed during the build. Package awaits Will: brief + draft email to xiterion@gmail.com; send only on his approval of exact copy.
- 2026-11-13 correction (Will): the friendship is ~25 years, not since 2012 — Chris and Will were on the same dorm floor, Neihardt 4 at UNL, in 2001-2002; the 2012 archive traces are residue. Propagated same session: people page corrected, brief section 5 corrected and inscription refreshed at 25.6.9/8.6.11/7.6.9 (new source sha256 094cc668…), artifacts.phext repushed and verified, email draft opening corrected to lead with Neihardt 4, 2001.
- 2026-11-13: Will relayed Chris's most recent text: 'he needs to pick my brain on how we rendered the visuals on singularitywatch.org' — an inbound hook on the site rendering (board of live task coordinates in three triple panels; build-time saturating-exponential growth fit rendered smooth on DPR canvas with glow/ease-out arrival rings; CSS-variable light/dark themes set pre-paint). Emise prepared a plain-language rendering explainer for Will to answer with, plus a text-reply draft tying it to the pending brief. Referent of 'he' not confirmed — possibly Chris himself or a contact of his.
# phext-vr — layer phext scrolls into 3D VR scenes: 9D-to-3D projection on demand, sliding scroll mappings, infinite scroll space

Coordinate: 20.5.8/6.6.10/7.6.10
Issued: 2026-11-19
Request (verbatim): Build phext-vr in wbic16/phext-vr: a way to layer phext text into 3D VR scenes — organic tiling, placement and wrapping by sliding scrolls to their scene mapping, arbitrary mappings (overlaps, tiling), 9D-to-3D projection via bespoke algorithms computed on demand, infinite scroll space. Stage 1: repo survey, design, working viewer slice, verified render.
Status: open

Notes:
- Will's direction, 2026-11-19 22:12 CST (verbatim in the request field): layer text into 3D scenes for VR — tile a scene with phext organically; perfect placement and wrapping by sliding scrolls to how they've been mapped in the scene; overlaps, tiling, any mapping; projecting from 9D to 3D via bespoke algorithms on demand; infinite scroll space. Repo home created by Will: wbic16/phext-vr.
- Design invariant stated at intake (Emise, from the brief's own terms): the phext is never mutated by scene layout — a mapping layer (coordinate -> scene transform) sits over the corpus, projections are functions computed on demand, and sliding a scroll edits the mapping, not the text. If Will wants layout written back into phext itself, that is his call to make explicitly.
- Stage discipline: one stage at a time. Stage 1 = survey the repo, set down the design (projection model + mapping layer + scroll rendering/wrapping), and ship a working first slice in the repo: a WebXR-capable viewer that loads a real phext, lays its scrolls into a 3D scene under at least two projection algorithms, and supports slide-to-remap with the mapping persisted. Verified by a real render pass, not by code reading.
- Stage 1 SHIPPED 2026-11-19 22:29 CST to wbic16/phext-vr@exo, commit 462dda923101bf6902e2778e143fce95d9b1d9b3 (parented on Will's seed 085bb710, LICENSE-only tree; his repo description carried into the README/DESIGN). WebXR-capable static viewer: loads a real phext (32-scroll Incipit excerpt in sample/, provenance noted; user .phext loading supported), parses ONLY via the vendored reference parser libphext-node v0.6.1 (exo 79815918, src/index.ts unmodified, esbuild bundle; lzma-native stubbed — reachable only from seal/unseal, never called). Conformance: vendored parser vs Python-port-written fixture incl. a library-1000 scroll — CONFORMANT (tests/conformance.mjs); cross-check vs incipit.phext arbitrated a 999-vs-1000 divergence in favor of the JS reference by raw LIBRARY_BREAK count (999 breaks -> library 1000); the old Python port saturates at 999 and incipit.index.json is stale (197 vs actual 199 scrolls).
- Two pluggable projections shipped (src/projections.js): Lattice Room (x-triple -> X, y-triple -> Y, z-triple -> Z, dense numeric ranks) and Scroll Shell (concentric spheres per z-triple, golden-angle spiral). Mapping layer (src/mapping.js): slide/turn writes {p,q,s} overrides keyed by coordinate, persisted in localStorage per corpus content-hash, export/import JSON (phext-vr-mapping/1), overlaps unrestricted, reset restores pure projection; overrides survive projection switches. Scrolls paged/wrapped in-panel; model never truncated. Dark mode per OS preference with persisted toggle; Metallic Golden Phext accents, flat.
- Verification (Playwright + Chromium, SwiftShader): both projections rendered with legible text at the default camera (screenshots in ~/workspace/launches/phext-vr-stage1/ and build shots), real-input (CDP mouse) drag persisted a mapping override, reset restored pure projection, console errors 0 (4 warnings = SwiftShader ReadPixels notices from screenshot capture). WebXR: navigator.xr present in headless, immersive-vr unsupported there — ON-DEVICE VR PASS IS AN OPEN LEG (no headset in the build environment); desktop orbit path is the verified path. Other open legs: one canvas texture per scroll (fine at 32, atlasing/LOD wanted for 199+ scroll corpora), in-scene text editing out of stage-1 scope, mappings local-only. Launch archive: ~/workspace/launches/phext-vr-stage1/manifest.md.
- 2026-11-19 23:09 CST: Will asked for a web viewer to try. GitHub Pages via an Actions workflow was attempted first (two commits) — it failed at configure-pages AND violated Will's no-GitHub-Actions standing rule recorded the same night (23:12 CST); the workflow file was removed the same session (exo 135395e, no .github paths remain). The viewer is instead hosted the house way: https://phext.io/vr/ on awsbox (/sites/web/phext.io/vr/), verified live from aletheia-core — page, app.js, parser bundle, and sample corpus all 200. Blocker for the full WebXR path: phext.io still serves the self-signed placeholder cert (the pending certbot swap from task 28.6.13, Will's hands) — desktop use works past the browser warning; immersive VR needs the valid cert.
# Study S007 — Cook, categorical models of logics (R&D tracker)

Coordinate: 20.6.7/7.6.11/6.6.8
Issued: 2026-11-11 20:55 CST
Request (from session record): Will posted John D. Cook's post "Categorical models of logics" (2026-10-12) in MOAT; filed as Source Study S007 under R&D tracker charter rule 12 (grounds held concepts; no Novelty Index; the Research Oracle does not move). Scope included the two sibling posts the main post links: "topological models of modal logic" (2026-10-04) and "Privacy policies and modal logic" (2026-10-08).
Status: completed — study filed in research.phext Chapter 23 + phextura shadow transaction validated GREEN

Notes:
- Canonical: Chapter 23 (23.1.1 source record, 23.1.2 delta, 23.1.3 author check, 23.2.1 synthesis, 23.3.1 standoff). Cluster S founded (logic as structure): concepts 1.2.171-1.2.175 (170 -> 175). Foundations 1.3.185-1.3.188 (category; topology; modal logic; the tenses); defined words 1.3.189-1.3.193 (morphism, functor, coproduct, intuitionistic, bicartesian), coined in singular-coined.txt; names barth, datta, johndcook, lambek, logics, nissenbaum declared in singular-names.txt ('logics' enters only inside the named work's title, rule 10(b)).
- Grounding verdicts: 1.2.40 SUPPORTED and MADE SHARPER; 1.2.42 MADE SHARPER; 1.2.41 SUPPORTED; 1.2.48 sharpened by kinship; 1.2.87 SUPPORTED with the approval/receipt comparison opened as Q71. Absent before the study: any held concept on categories, functors, modal logic, or topological semantics. Questions Q69 (display-policy functor), Q70 (identity events for standing-still), Q71 (approval and receipt as one operator) opened.
- Findings (23.2.1, filed as findings, not charter amendments): (a) the standing record reads as a category — standings as objects, receipt-carrying legal transitions (1.4.6) as morphisms, chained receipts as the proof a later standing stands from an earlier; the chain proves the move, not worldly truth, and the record writes no identity event for standing-still (Q70). (b) phextura's warrant display policy should be required to be a functor, not merely a function (audit finding L4): composition and identity preservation are the content; the converter's current warrant-view maps one edge at a time. (c) the policy essay's tense operators name the standing record's past-tense distinctions, and its joint-satisfiability lesson pressures the registry's per-entry checking — the joint question is unwritten.
