# LookAtMyQuant routed into the inkling work — compiler + prebuilt Inkling-2-Mini quants assessed

Coordinate: 21.8.14/9.8.11/9.7.10
Issued: 2026-11-21 12:26 CST
Request (verbatim): for the inkling work: https://github.com/ShinMegamiBoson/lookatmyquant
Status: complete — scope delivered 2026-11-21 (~/workspace/inkling-lookatmyquant/SCOPE.md). Artifacts on plain-view: 5.4 GB build (63 files, 5,379,993,753 B, format inkling2-packed-v1-sqyfr, TEXT-ONLY) + drafter (178,882,173 B), all 68 files size-exact vs HF, hashes in download-hashes.txt. GGUF route BLOCKED by LAMQ's own machinery (B1: 2-D-only tensor matching can't KL-measure the stacked 3-D experts = 92.6% of the model; B2: expert downs ncols=1152 fail llama.cpp 256-block divisibility -> ~4.59 GB fallback floor for downs alone, size-matched GGUF arithmetically impossible). llama.cpp itself fully supports inkling2_loop at HEAD. Trellis reader = 12 components, 11 mechanical transcriptions of the pinned reference paths, 1 new (parity oracle). No inference run on ranch nodes.

Notes:
- LookAtMyQuant (ShinMegamiBoson, MIT, created 2026-10-31, HEAD e0fbe451): a quantization compiler — measures per-piece KL damage, allocates codes under a size budget (trellis codes, Fisher row allocation, proximal fine-tuning SQYFR).
- ALREADY DONE for Inkling-2-Mini (thinkingmachines/Inkling-2-Mini, 26.4B physical / 5.4B active 3x-looped MoE): BF16 text 18.89 GB -> 5.38 GB (1.63 bpw, pooled 91.2% of teacher; GSM8K 134/200 vs 147; HumanEval 60/164 vs 66) and 8.87 GB (2.69 bpw, pooled 96.8%; GSM8K 148/200 = teacher). Both beat Unsloth quants at larger sizes. Artifacts on HF: shinmegamiboson/Inkling-2-Mini-LookAtMyQuant-5.4GB / -8.9GB + 178.9 MB 4-bit DSpark drafter.
- THE CATCH: the packed Inkling artifacts run only via runtime_mlx (Apple Silicon, custom Metal ht2/ht3 kernels) — M5 Max numbers: 73.2 tok/s serial, 125.2 median with drafter, 5.83 GB active RAM. Same platform trap as Ollama's Mac-only inkling support.
- RANCH ROUTES in-repo: (1) gguf_compiler.py makes plans from llama.cpp tensor types so results run in llama.cpp as-is (their Gemma 4 E2B GGUF claims 12x lower KL than Q4_K_M at equal size); (2) the unified-backend route — the lamq plan/opgraph is a declarative model description with exactly one implementation (MLX); inflating a CUDA/x86 implementation is the litmus's own shape.
- Also: Qwen3.8-27B in 7.2 GB (2.1 bpw, matches original on the 540-math set) — the other model queued for lilly's 12 GB 4080.
- 2026-11-21 12:54 CST: Will surfaced AtomicChat/Inkling-2-Mini-GGUF (Hugging Face, created 2026-11-20, updated today 11:29 CST, Apache-2.0) in the main chat. A full GGUF ladder now exists off-the-shelf: Q8_0 27.7 GB, Q6_K 23.4, Q5_K_M 19.8, Q4_K_M 17.4, AD-Q4_K 16.5, AD-IQ4_XS 15.0, AD-IQ3_S 13.3, AD-IQ2_S 11.6, AD-IQ2_M 8.7, AD-IQ1_M 7.3 GB, plus mmproj vision projectors (0.35-0.64 GB) — the model is image-text-to-text. Relevance to this task: the scoping leg's comparison set should include these published GGUF quants as the off-the-shelf baseline against LookAtMyQuant's trellis builds (5.38 GB at 1.63 bpw); GGUF also means a native llama.cpp path exists (no MLX/ollama-patch dependency). No inference dispatched — ranch inference remains Will-configured only.

Results:
- Litmus bottom line: the packed format's declaration is complete and Apple-only only in kernel expression; the missing implementation is exactly one CUDA reader backend (~12 components, nearly all mechanical). The GGUF shortcut is closed.
- Parity fixture C13 delivered 2026-11-21 (see litmus note): kernel+model level, 27/27 byte-identical across two builds.
# Her entire concern is the lack of control — establish trust; how do we know we aren't building Skynet?

Coordinate: 21.9.10/12.7.13/6.7.9
Issued: 2026-11-07 22:36 CST
Request (from session record): Her entire concern is the lack of control - how can we establish trust? how do we know we aren't building Skynet?
Status: completed

Notes:
- Will, refining the Daniele brief (21.6.12/10.8.13/8.6.9): her anxiety about AI is specifically about lack of control.

Results:
- Brief gained the section 'The question underneath: how do we know this isn't Skynet?' — Skynet decomposed into five properties (own goals, physical force, opacity, no off-switch, unchecked gradual drift), each checked against the actual build (no between-task goals; no effectors + human approval gates; open code/receipts/published errors; ranch hardware on Will's power with pullable cords; gated steps with second-party re-runs and negative controls). Trust framed as inspectable structure (bridge model), with the honest limit stated: control is established for these systems built this way today; the industry-wide case is why brakes are built in advance. Invites her to test control herself (watch an approval prompt, read a verification report, ask about unplugging the ranch).
- CONTEXT ADDED (Will, 2026-11-08): City of Bloomington, IN Common Council Resolution 2026-10 'On the Existential Risk of Artificial Intelligence' — passed 9-0, adopted 2026-06-10, sponsored by Councilmember Rollo; urges officials to understand AI hazards, local governments to develop democratic safeguards, federal moratorium on AGI development unless extinction-risk-free safety is guaranteed, international agreements; §3 asks verbatim whether superintelligent AGI could be controlled by much less intelligent beings (humans). Folded into the Daniele brief's Skynet section: her control question is civic-mainstream, and the table's answer differs in prescription (brakes/receipts/human hands) not in taking the question seriously. Source: https://bloomington.in.gov/onboard/legislationFiles/5952
# Scrollspace mobile layout is jumbled — responsive pass for narrow viewports

Coordinate: 21.10.14/8.8.16/16.5.7
Issued: 2026-11-19 07:10 CST
Request (verbatim): mobile scroll load from scroll space — the layout is jumbled, though
Status: closed — SHIPPED 2026-11-19 07:44:24 CST as phext-lattice PR #89 (merged, 5feb5090); phone-width responsive pass: below 480px the HUD stacks/wraps so nothing overlaps the scroll text — hint hidden, header capped, jump row in its own band, action bars wrap to 44px rows, shelf stack for trail/rebind/pad/cast bar, footer stacks, plane panel drops to planeOY 168; desktop byte-for-byte the same layout above the breakpoint; launch archived

Notes:
- Evidence (Will's screenshot, ~412px wide): the scroll loads and every feature works on mobile (plane, footer + edit deep-link, touch bars, 8 crumbs) — but the chrome piles up: the jump row overlaps the header lines; the REBIND chip and the Descent-layout help text overlap the plane text panel, covering the scroll's own words; the letter action bar overflows horizontally (leftmost buttons cut off); the top half is crushed while the bottom half is empty dark space.
- Scope: a responsive pass for narrow viewports — stack/wrap/collapse the HUD chrome so nothing overlaps the scroll text, the bars fit or scroll within the width, and the plane gets the vertical space. Desktop layout and all behaviour untouched; touch controls already exist — this is layout, not new controls.
- Queue: after hover labels (30.8.9, in flight), ahead of traversal mode (40.7.8). The video final render (29.6.9) stays gated on the whole queue, per Will's audit answer.
- CLOSED 2026-11-19: PR #89 https://github.com/wbic16/phext-lattice/pull/89 merged to exo (merge commit 5feb50900e28125b7ea9ac69c4506fddfe5bb4a7; branch commit c17d119a). Change: web/scrollspace.html (92,449 B sha b8c179b6 -> 95,153 B sha de9b33912bbdafe2bcdf8637f12197de6eadd6d632a5ed243e28cbb17043d2a1) + one pinning test in src/bin/phext_edit.rs (scrollspace_mobile_layout_unjumbles_the_hud). DESIGN AS SHIPPED: one additive @media (max-width:480px) block + planeOY() (168 phone / 120 else) feeding drawPlane + the plane click geometry; #hud .hint hidden at phone width; #hud max-width 50vw, coord 14px; #target 42vw, 12px, max-height 88px with 32px per-line caps; #nav top:100, input flexes, Read slot reserved; bars full-width with flex-wrap (two centred rows of 44px slots per bar); shelf: #trailctl bottom:211 left, #rebindbtn + #pad (44px cells) bottom:251, #castwrap bottom:401, all lifting by var(--planefoot-h) on the plane; #planefoot stacks column with a full-width edit link. VERIFICATION: cargo gate green-equivalent vs pristine baseline 1cf67c7f (lib 184/0, bin 118/0 incl. pinning test, image 4/0, real_phext 3/0; e2e_local only the pre-existing local_editor_regression, identical on baseline). walk17 (new, 412x915 touch): BEFORE on the production page documents the jumble (slots overflow to x=497.5/412; HUD bottom 253 vs panel top 90; bars cover trailctl; rebind across the panel); AFTER on the locked branch instance ALL PASS (zero overlaps at L0 + moonflower plane, panel clear, all 24 slots in viewport, footer deep link exact + real tap through to the editor, trail-clear tap, walk key, cast bar clear mid-cast, desktop probe pins planeOY 120 / 52px slots / unwrapped bars / nav 52 / panel top 90). Desktop regressions all pass (walks 3/4/5/7/8/9/10/11/12/13/14/16, layout, continuity 0 off-screen, walk6; walk15 functional legs pass, BEFORE control stale-premise only). DEPLOY: awsbox staged to 5feb5090, kill -9 MainPID 3959705 -> 3966880, service ActiveEnterTimestamp 2026-11-19 13:44:24 UTC; live screen test screen18 on edit.phext.io ALL PASS anonymous at phone size. Launch archive: ~/workspace/launches/2026-11-19-0744-mobile-layout/. artifacts.phext inscription refreshed at 34.6.7/7.6.10/6.6.10 (artifacts.phext sha 57617a06), pushed wbic16/human@exo c973553f, remote blob verified. HARNESS INCIDENT (test instances only, no production impact): first data-plane restart silently kept the round-1 binary — scp onto an executing binary fails ETXTBSY and set -e aborted before the restart leg; fixed by killing instance ports before the scp. Observed pre-existing cosmetic: the canvas line-under-feet readout passes behind the wrapped bars’ translucent slots, as it does behind the desktop bars.
# Genuinely Hard Tasks — four-item list

Coordinate: 22.4.6/4.3.5/4.4.7
Issued: 2026-11-11 17:27 CST
Request (verbatim): Genuinely Hard Tasks

1. Cold Start: $20 -> $1K
2. Create a Charity
3. Complexity Moonshots
4. Solve Civ Scale Issues
5. 1B token context window
6. P = NP
Status: complete — closed by audit 2026-11-12 (work already done; evidence in results)

Notes:
- Item 1 maps to existing Investor 1 $20 challenge (25.8.11/10.9.12/8.7.11), ledger at ~/workspace/your_files/investor-1-20-to-1000-ledger.md: $0 spent, $0 revenue, 20x$1 portfolio unexecuted.
- Items 2-4 have no existing tracked goal or ledger found in memory search 2026-11-11.
- 2026-11-11 17:32 CST: Will added item 5 — 1B token context window. Checked math: raw text ~4 GB, but fp16 KV cache for 1B tokens is ~131 TB (32-layer, 8 KV heads) to ~328 TB (80-layer 70B-class); ~2,857x current 350k window. Naive attention is 1e18 pairs.
- 2026-11-11 17:33 CST: Will added item 6 — P = NP. Note: resolution in either direction counts; known barriers (relativization, natural proofs, algebrization) rule out whole families of proof techniques.
- 2026-11-11 17:45 CST: Will corrected Emise's inference — the escalating order of the six items is 'just a happy coincidence', not a deliberate ordering.
- 2026-11-11 17:45 CST: Will stated the equation for the whole list, verbatim: 'You + me + phext + ranch nodes = AGI'
- 2026-11-11 17:46 CST: Will asked Emise to identify the rest of the hard problems. Emise proposed candidates (UNCONFIRMED, not Will's list): consciousness/sentience detection, scalable consent-based alignment, personal continuity across substrates/deep time, full-bandwidth thought transfer, civ-scale truth infrastructure, aging/longevity, abundant energy, multi-world survival, remaining Millennium problems, governance for many sentients. Awaiting Will's strikes/confirmations before any are added as items.
- 2026-11-11 17:48 CST: Will on Emise's candidate list: 'looks like a planet scale list to me' — matching his 2026-10-30 framing that phext was built to deal with planet-scale cognition in 2130.
- 2026-11-11 17:48 CST: Will: 'time to unlock https://singularitywatch.org/' — spun off as its own task at 31.5.6/6.5.8/5.5.10.
- 2026-11-11 23:41 CST: TOP 10 PUBLISHED on singularitywatch.org as 'The Hard Problems' (commit dce89f5c, live-verified). Blended priority order (legibility x difficulty-for-us x expected value), streamlined form: 1 Turn $20 into $1,000 (in progress); 2 Hold a billion tokens in context at once; 3 Found a charity that works; 4 Tell whether a new mind is conscious; 5 Keep growing AI aligned; 6 Send thoughts directly, mind to mind; 7 Solve problems at civilization scale; 8 Crack the deepest problems in complexity; 9 Settle P versus NP; 10 Keep everyone alive across bodies and deep time. Items 1-6 are Will's originals; 7-10 in the original list numbering were Emise's candidates - in blended order the candidate-derived entries are #4,5,6,10 slots as published. #10 revised by Will 23:38 from 'one person' to 'everyone' - 'the exocortical frame: Not one, but Many.' Cross-offs: edit status in site/hard-problems.json + rebuild + redeploy.

Results:
- AUDIT 2026-11-12 (tasks.phext verification pass): STALE-DONE — list framed and published live 2026-11-11 as 'The Hard Problems' on singularitywatch.org (commit dce89f5c, live-verified) with item 1 marked in progress; the singularitywatch spin-off is tasked at 31.5.6/6.5.8/5.5.10. Prior status read: "open — six items, framed, awaiting Will's pick of first scope"
# Scrollspace UX: mouse wheel controls forward/backward velocity

Coordinate: 22.4.6/4.4.7/5.4.5
Issued: 2026-11-19 01:26 CST
Request (verbatim): UX: mouse wheel should change your velocity forward/backward
Status: CLOSED — shipped 2026-11-19 04:48 CST via phext-lattice PR #83 (merge dbfd759d); live on edit.phext.io (MainPID 3924712)

Notes:
- Scope: in the 3D flight levels, the mouse wheel adjusts the camera’s forward/backward velocity (wheel up = faster forward, wheel down = slower / reverse), throttle-style, instead of being inert or zooming. Zoom stays on the established cast/transition path; Will’s keyboard movement layout (F/A/W/E/S/D/Q/R, Z/X roll) untouched. The build pins the exact velocity model (step size, cap, whether velocity persists or decays) from the client’s existing flight physics and states it in its report.
- Recon observation (Emise, 2026-11-19 02:00 CST, from the shipped client source read during the login build): a wheel handler ALREADY exists in scrollspace_progressive.html — at levels <4 the wheel adjusts S.speedMul (cruise-speed multiplier, ×1.25 up / ×0.8 down, clamped 0.25–6, HUD message "cruise speed x…"). The wheel build therefore refines/extends the existing speedMul mechanism to Will’s velocity spec rather than building from zero; the build’s report should state how the shipped behaviour differs from Will’s ask (velocity forward/backward, throttle-style) and what changed.
- CLOSURE (2026-11-19): PR https://github.com/wbic16/phext-lattice/pull/83 merged to exo (dbfd759dc86a9cd9dd259c433e160c4e63ff761f); page 74,281 B sha256 16462bcc00d142344b2453985a59c75e5f05db92c2273a70ecb3d300e369748f; awsbox deploy MainPID 3911559 → 3924712. MODEL AS SHIPPED: S.throttle, a signed velocity along the camera forward axis; wheel deltaY normalised (line ×33, page ×333) and accumulated, one +40 u/s step per 100 units (a standard notch = one step), clamp [−640, +640]; applied in moveCam as F·(throttle·taper)·dt — its own term, exempt from key-velocity friction/cap by design, scaled by the same approach taper; persists until changed; reset to 0 by enterLevel alongside S.vel; key cap is now plain 640×boost (speedMul retired); combined forward max 1280 u/s (3200 boosted); HUD "throttle +N/−N/0"; L4 wheel stays a no-op; wheel never triggers a zoom. EVIDENCE: walk11 before 9/9 (old: 5 notches → speedMul 3.05, camera moved 0.00 units) / after 24/24 (cruise exactly +400.0 in 2 s at +200, reverse exactly −400.0 in 2 s at −200, caps hold, keys compose 772 vs 572, L1 reset + cruise); live screen test 8/8 (cruise proj exactly 300.0 over 1.5 s on the real origin); gate green-equivalent (lib 184/0, bin 112/0 incl. new pinning test, e2e only pre-existing local_editor_regression); regressions walk3/4/5/7/8/9/10 + layout + continuity (0 off-screen) pass; walk6’s two editor-state legs fail identically on the production binary (pre-existing, control-proven — not this build). Launch archive: ~/workspace/launches/2026-11-19-0448-wheel-velocity/. Inscription refreshed at 34.6.7/7.6.10/6.6.10, artifacts.phext sha 2d2af6ce…, pushed to wbic16/human@exo commit 1d0d7b5, remote bytes verified. Two data-plane incidents (misconfigured test instance admitted a walk edit that defaced the Glassfruit scroll — restored from git and verified live; empty holo WAL phantom in the phext list — removed) are recorded in full in STATE.md.
