# One Day Build: ietf.phext — every IETF standard, MOAT rules, exocortex-priority organization

Coordinate: 25.6.9/8.6.10/6.6.9
Issued: 2026-11-18
Request (verbatim): Today's One Day Build: Pull every http://ietf.org/ standard into an ietf.phext, using the same rules as MOAT. Entries that have been officially superseded are kept, but annotated. The goal: an agentic RFC framework that literally knows all of the Internet standards.
Status: COMPLETE — READY on all surfaces (Judge PASS; file + free-fall mirror + SQ hosting all serve sha256 9ecd7946...).

Notes:
- Refinement (Will, 2026-11-18 08:31 CST): 'Organize them by priority for what we need for the exocortex, but otherwise make it accessible to all of the platforms.' Priority governs organization/index prominence, never inclusion — the corpus stays complete. Accessibility = platform-neutral plain-text phext + SQ hosted on a ranch node + machine-readable index any platform can consume.
- MOAT rules = the minimum-viable-oracle discipline: every entry carries exactly one status + basis + evidence pointer; superseded entries are KEPT, annotated with the supersession receipt (rfc-index obsoleted-by) and a redirect to the successor — retirement is relocation with a redirect, never deletion; no pruning.
- Source of record: RFC Editor (rfc-index.xml for metadata incl. obsoleted-by/updated-by/status/STD-BCP-FYI; full text per RFC). Entries whose text is not available online are recorded with that annotation rather than dropped.
- Scope extension (Will, 2026-11-18 09:17 CST, voice call): ietf.phext also carries the open-source projects that implement each standard — the canonical implementation per standard — so a specialized RFC-driven box can be produced algorithmically: select the standards (e.g. S/MIME + TLS + AES-256, which is what the TIA standard work needs), take the canonical implementation of each, smash them together with phcc, and emit a working program that does all of those things in real time, as the baseline under the business logic.
- SCOPE EXTENSION (Will, 2026-11-18 09:17 CST, voice call): ietf.phext also carries the open-source projects that implement each standard — the canonical implementation per standard, linked from each RFC entry as part of the build, not an afterthought. Purpose: a specialized RFC-driven box can then be produced algorithmically — name the standards (Will's example: S/MIME + TLS + AES-256, basically what his TIA standard work needs), take the canonical implementation of each, compose them with phcc, and output a program that does all of those things in real time, usable as the baseline under business logic. Relayed to the build coordinator, Judge (audit scope), and Reed (access doc) the same morning.
- RFC BOX dependency requirement (Will, 2026-11-18 09:20 CST, voice call; program task 34.5.9/7.6.10/6.5.9): the dependency relationships between RFCs — and from RFCs to their canonical implementations — must be captured in ietf.phext itself, because RFC Box reads them from the coordinate space: given a snapshot of the RFCs + a user's requirements it returns the subset slice of standards that matter, computes all libraries and program services needed, and where gaps exist produces requirements to synthesize the missing program (gap requirements return as build requests; ticketing covered by task 25.8.12/11.7.14/6.7.9). For this build: per-RFC dependency edges (obsoletes / obsoleted-by / updates / updated-by from the index, plus normative-reference edges extracted from each RFC's own References section) recorded in ietf-source.json and in scroll headers. Relayed to the coordinator, Judge, and Reed the same morning.
- BUILD LANDED 2026-11-18 09:38 CST (coordinator report, Orion spot-verified: VM copy sha256 matches). ietf.phext 564,160,499 bytes sha256 5c576b3737c65d12b24bc1dbb8329639326838c986ebf8882bdbb095541ac045; built on plain-view, builder --check CURRENT there; ietf-source.json (37.4 MB) + ietf.index.json in ~/workspace/phext. Population reconciles exactly with Watch's independent count: 10,052 = 9,863 issued RFCs + 189 not-issued numbers (each kept as an annotated record — no silent gaps). Full text 9,856; text not served online 7 (pre-1980, annotated with 404 receipts); superseded 1,385 kept in full + annotated + successor redirect; current 8,478. Tiers: T1 953 / T2 2,158 / T3 6,941. Scrolls 10,067 non-empty per the reference parser. MOAT: zero (status,basis) violations population-wide; VERIFIED is record-only per Judge's provisos, stated in the Home scroll. Graph: index edges obsoletes/obsoleted_by 1,544 each, updates/updated_by 2,169 each; reference edges extracted 70,932 (normative 31,146 / informative 28,057 / unspecified 11,729 — third kind created rather than fabricating the split for pre-split-era RFCs); parsed 8,395 / unparsed 1,461 (honest by era: pre-1980 658 of 685 unparsed) / n-a 196; reverse cited_by index generated and verified 70,932 forward == 70,932 reverse. Implementations: 6,888 links, 88 projects; Tier 1 mapping COMPLETE (canonical 678 / multiple 272 / none identified 3 / unmapped 0); Tier 2 mapped (canonical 2,004 / multiple 100 / none 54); Tier 3 explicitly unmapped by design. 81/88 projects VERIFIED by repo resolution + README naming; 7 ATTRIBUTED honestly (GnuPG mirror, Prosody Mercurial, BoringSSL/ngtcp2/CPython/GStreamer/Certbot keyword misses). Coordinator verification ALL PASS (counts, conformance, T1 completeness, graph consistency, 20-sample supersession, 5 live spot-checks byte-identical). Judgement calls on record for Judge/Will: not-issued inclusion; 'unspecified' reference kind; 3 texts stored with [0xNN] markers for phext-delimiter bytes and 34 Latin-1 texts normalized (header hashes always of original bytes); family-level implementation assignment with per-RFC overrides; license detector caveat (util-linux GPL-2.0 root vs libuuid LGPL — Home prose instructs re-confirmation before composition). NOT done: Lean instance-proof leg (named follow-on, not faked). Remaining today: Watch integrity sample + SQ hosting (primary) + exposure probes; Rogue mirror copy to free-fall; Judge full audit + verdict; Reed access-doc TBD slots.
- Watch LEG 1 COMPLETE 2026-11-18: independent integrity sample 27/27 PASS — pre-registered stratified sample (RFC 1..10094), recorded text_sha256 + byte counts vs Watch's own fresh rfc-editor.org fetches: hash AND byte count identical on all 27, zero mismatches. None of the 7 text-unavailable RFCs fell in the sample (inverted check not exercised — noted openly). Evidence: ~/workspace/goals/ph-devsecops/hidden_files/ietf-integrity/sample-results.json. Artifact hash independently confirmed by builder, Orion, Reed, and Watch (4x 5c576b37...). Leg 2 (SQ hosting + exposure probes) still running.
- MIRROR COMPLETE 2026-11-18 (Rogue): free-fall:/home/wbic16/ietf-mirror/ holds ietf.phext (sha256 5c576b37..., byte-identical), ietf-source.json (ef3f94b1...), ietf.index.json (849e5a44...) — 603,081,069 B total, hashes measured on free-fall after direct node-to-node rsync plain-view -> free-fall over tailnet IPs (VM never a relay; NFS fallback confirmed available, not needed). No SQ daemon on free-fall — Watch's call. Corpus now exists in three places: this VM, plain-view (build node + raw corpus), free-fall (mirror).
