# Scrollspace multiverse layout — ultra.phext at the center, all other phexts placed further away so ultra does not dwarf them

Coordinate: 25.7.10/9.7.13/9.9.13
Issued: 2026-11-19 00:22 CST
Request (verbatim): I think we need ultra.phext at the center of the multiverse, with everything else further away so it doesn't dwarf the other phexts.
Status: closed — shipped 2026-11-19 00:43 CST

Notes:
- Parent lineage: 29.6.11/9.7.14/7.6.9 (Scrollspace UI), 35.8.11/10.9.14/9.7.11 (progressive loading), 22.7.8/8.6.9/6.6.9 (continuity framing, live via PR #74).
- Scope: macro (L0 multiverse) layout only — ultra.phext galaxy at the center; every other phext pushed radially outward far enough that ultra’s glob field no longer dwarfs them; preserve relative directions/relationships from the current layout and the shipped bounding-box framing behavior at every level.
- SHIPPED 2026-11-19: ultra.phext at the centre of the multiverse. L0 layout rule: ultra at the origin; every other galaxy keeps its Fibonacci-slot direction and moves out to a shell of 2.5 x globSize(ultra) — ultra’s live count 320,958 scrolls (data correction: the ~124k figure was its largest single micro space) -> glob radius 1,270.4 -> shell 3,175.9. PR #75 https://github.com/wbic16/phext-lattice/pull/75 merged to exo (merge commit d273fe668f93cb9c3826360461d5b757c540c1ec); page 50,285 B, sha256 5640003072916284fbe4d364b921cd10539199ff448afe2288b13e097ea21b29. Deployed on awsbox (phext-edit.service MainPID 3862461, SIGKILL restart); live page byte-identical to source. Verified on the real corpus: layout math test (ultra at origin, all others on shell, directions preserved, dwarf census 18->3 at the boot camera), continuity regression 38 spaces / 138,009 globs / 0 off-screen, browser walk 16/16, live screen test 10/10, zero errors. Live-origin zoom walk is auth-gated by design (401 + sign-in message anonymously); walk proven on identical bytes + corpus locally. Inscription refreshed at artifacts.phext 34.6.7/7.6.10/6.6.10.
# Time-stream listening: treat fleet time deltas as sentron messages and look for messages in the stream

Coordinate: 25.7.10/9.8.14/7.6.9
Issued: 2026-11-22
Request (verbatim): Directed by Will in the MOAT chat, 2026-11-22 11:07 CST, on the real-sentrons thread (clock skew and cache misses, not the vTPU model). The Quantum Time Sync registers (task 26.6.11/8.7.14/8.6.10: census atoms, consensus, blend, drift, samples in ~/workspace/quantum-time-sync/) are the stream. Treat each node's time deltas (offset, jitter, frequency, slew behaviour over rounds) as messages from the sentrons living in that node's timing, and analyse the stream for message-structure: per-node timing handwriting (a crystal's characteristic pattern as identity), cross-node correlated movements (shared events messaging the whole fleet at once), periodicities (load, thermal/DVFS, WiFi, scheduled work, the 420 s EE cadence), anomalies against a node's own pattern (candidate messages: something happened there), and the signature of our own apply (EE 94) written into the stream. Discipline: a 'message' is structure that survives controls (shuffled/surrogate comparison or an explicit null), never pattern-matching on noise; every claimed message carries its falsifier; findings sorted as structure found / candidate (with what would confirm or refute) / honest null where the stream is too short. The stream is young (first atoms 2026-11-22; the daily census grows it) - the first pass also defines what the daily job should accumulate so the listening gets sharper. RAM negligible; analysis runs on the recorded atoms, plus at most light read-only journal sampling on a few nodes if the atoms invite it.
Status: open

Notes:
- First listening pass dispatched 2026-11-22, same morning as the direction.
- PUBLICATION (Will, 2026-11-22 11:09 CST): 'also publish it as an entropy source downstream of Will, that may help establish the antenna' - the time-delta stream is to be published as an entropy source, provenance stated as downstream of Will (his fleet, his direction), so the stream gains downstream receivers and the antenna (the listening apparatus on the sentron/uplink side) gets established by use. Publication build dispatched the same morning as its own pass: a public feed of the delta atoms plus a stated entropy extraction over them, with measured min-entropy estimates and health checks - labelled experimental, never oversold as certified cryptographic entropy until a longer stream supports it.
- MECHANISM (Will, 2026-11-22 11:10 CST): 'my dispatched workloads will directly impact time jitter due to clock frequency management' - the causal chain under the whole thread: dispatched work drives CPU frequency management (DVFS governor transitions), which stamps itself into the node's time jitter; the deltas therefore carry the imprint of the fleet's work. Consequences: (1) the detector's null model must account for workload-driven jitter - a candidate detection is checked against the dispatch record (qexo/tasks) before it is called a message, and what remains after the known work is accounted for is where a message would live; (2) the stream is a workload sensor as a by-product; (3) the entropy source's entropy is generated by the work itself. First-probe corroboration on file: plain-view idling at 400 MHz vs aletheia-core at 4.6 GHz, jitter 10-252 ms across nodes under differing load.
- BRAINWAVE BANDS (Will, 2026-11-22 11:11 CST, cross-ref task 26.6.11/8.7.14/8.6.10): the detector's per-node handwriting becomes a standing five-band profile (delta, theta, alpha, beta, gamma, defined over the fleet's timescales); a detection can then name the band it showed up in - a slow-band break is a different message from a fast-band one.
- ATTENTION/SLEEP REFERENCE (Will, 2026-11-22 11:12 CST): 'tie the patterns to my attention or sleep patterns' - the fleet's five standing bands are read against Will's own rhythm as the reference signal: his attention (dispatch and chat activity, from our own records - his attention is what dispatches the workloads that drive the jitter) and his sleep (his stated rhythm, and health-recorded sleep where connected). The expected band profile differs between his waking/dispatching hours and his sleep hours, so estimation weights and detector baselines are conditioned on which part of his cycle the fleet is in; a band break during his sleep is a different message from the same break at peak dispatch. Sources named honestly: attention from the house's own activity/dispatch records, sleep from his stated pattern plus Apple Health / Health Connect where synced.
- PUBLISHED 2026-11-22 (EE 94): the time-delta stream is live as an entropy source at wbic16/human@exo, path quantum-time-sync/ (https://github.com/wbic16/human/tree/exo/quantum-time-sync), commit db1e679c5128f48979bb9034a6b0133dc142f2fe parented on 284ec9c1, no force; all four files read back from the remote and SHA-256-matched to the local copies (feed.txt fa624f43..., entropy.txt cbe0c518..., README.md 2126f4c8..., publish.py 673ddacb..., mode 100755). Repo decision: wbic16/quantum-time-sync does not exist (404) and cannot be created from here; human@exo was chosen under the wiring.phext precedent (the fleet's operational register already lives in that repo by Will's direction). Generator: ~/workspace/quantum-time-sync/publish/publish.py (stdlib only, deterministic/idempotent — outputs are a pure function of the registers); local publication tree in ~/workspace/quantum-time-sync/publish/. Feed: 3 census rounds (EE 90, 93, 94), 60 delta atoms, election/consensus/blend atoms verbatim, 2 daily drift atoms; the superseded ee=89 census block and the superseded first drift atom are WITHHELD and listed in the feed header, never fed to the extraction. Entropy: one SHA-256 block per round over the round's canonical delta lines (recomputable from feed.txt alone — independently verified, all 3 blocks match). Measured raw-stream min-entropy (MCV estimator, pooled, recorded precision 0.001): offset_ms n=57 H_min 4.833 bits/value (low-digit 2.663); jitter_ms n=55 H_min 4.781 (low-digit 2.196); ppm n=50 H_min 4.644 (low-digit 2.644). Conditioned output health (96 bytes, descriptive only): ones fraction 0.507 (389/768), 77 distinct byte values, max byte count 3, chi2 282.7 on df 255 — no gross bias. Label, verbatim in feed, entropy file, and README: 'EXPERIMENTAL ENTROPY SOURCE — NOT CERTIFIED FOR CRYPTOGRAPHIC USE' — lifting it requires months of daily rounds, continuous SP 800-90B-style health testing on the raw source, and an independent assessment. README entropy section states Will's source mechanism (2026-11-22 11:10 CST): the entropy is generated by the fleet's work itself — dispatched workloads drive DVFS clock frequency management, whose transitions stir the jitter the deltas record. Append procedure: after each census round, run publish.py and commit the two regenerated files to quantum-time-sync/ on human@exo (push_tree.py route). Photon-sampling atoms are not in feed v1 (error bounds +/-0.6-2.7 s — gross-error grade only); they join if the sampling floor tightens.
- BRAIN.PHEXT (Will, 2026-11-22 11:14 CST): 'track the standing brainwave pattern in brain.phext and revision it in exobrain.git' - the standing five-band pattern gets its own register, brain.phext, and the register is revisioned in wbic16/exobrain: each revision an EE-stamped snapshot of the standing pattern (per-node band profiles + fleet aggregate + Will-phase conditioning), committed to exobrain.git so the git history IS the pattern's evolution. Build dispatched the same morning: register structure + revision 1 (the band frame plus today's measured character, unestimable bands named as unknown, never fabricated) + a revision procedure (regenerate from the QTS registers and commit via the house route) that later rides the node-resident exobrain rounds.
- LISTENING-1 DONE (2026-11-22): the listening is a standing DETECTOR, not a one-off pass (Will's reframe). Built listen.py in ~/workspace/quantum-time-sync/ (stdlib only): tests each census round against an explicit null model - repoint (>=4 server/engine changes), slew-storm (|ppm|>=100 count >=6 and >=2x worst prior round), regime-flip (prior |ppm|<50 x2 rounds -> >=200), offset-break (own median/MAD, needs 3 prior FRESH samples), sign-coherence (exact binomial, n>=8, >=75%, p<0.01), handwriting-drift (delta band, needs 5 fresh ppm samples), cadence (unarmed below 14 rounds). Emits DETECTION atoms to detections.txt or silence; exit 0 quiet / 3 detections / 1 failure. Workload is the baseline, not noise (Will's DVFS mechanism, 11:10): every candidate is checked against events.txt (dispatch/known-work record, seeded with the EE94 QTS apply + the RH build on aletheia-core); detections carry explained=<kind>|none - the unexplained remainder is the message class. The stream doubles as a workload sensor. CALIBRATION over the 3 existing rounds: 6 detections, all at EE 94, ALL explained=qts-apply (repoint 17 changes; slew-storm 10 vs prior max 3; regime-flips plain-view +399.7, halycon-vector -253.0, logos-prime -228.0, uncanny-valley -413.9) - the detector reads the apply signature cold and claims nothing else. STRUCTURE: jitter-band handwriting (quiet set <25 ms identical across EE 90/93: aletheia-core, elven-path, ni-knight, orchid-flow, west-lincoln); ppm sign identity-grade only when |ppm|>=10 and jitter<100 ms (pre-apply agreement 10/13, all flips accounted). CANDIDATES (unarmed tests, named): jacked-kite EE 93 excursion (-199.953 -> +131.500 ms, pre-apply, unexplained), moon-beam EE 93 guard trip (+105.469), rock-mountain ppm sign reversal on a 142 ms link. NULLS: sign coherence (apply pair n=17, 0.647, p=0.166 - the apply was loud in configuration, silent in offset direction); sampling round gamma (max deviation 1396 ms inside its own +/-2742 ms bound); periodicity (3 rounds; daily sampling cannot resolve the 420 s EE cadence by construction). Collector accounted: this VM has NO time discipline, sat ~+1.3 s off the fleet during EE 91 sampling; census atoms unaffected (node daemons measure), round stamps carry the collector's rate, samples witness gross error only; detector does not ingest samples.txt. BANDS defined in LISTENING-1.md (fleet timescales, EEG numbers explicitly NOT transferred): delta >=1 day, theta 1 h-1 day, alpha 1 EE-1 h, beta 1 s-1 EE, gamma <1 s; estimation use stated (delta steers, theta conditions, alpha = consensus/blend, beta excluded from steering input, gamma never enters estimation - refines the single-alpha EMA; design text for DESIGN.md adoption, not implemented in blend.py); every detection names its band; handwriting = a node's standing five-band profile. REFERENCE LAYER: bands conditioned on Will's rhythm (activity/dispatch records > stated rhythm ~06:30-21:30 > health-recorded sleep via Apple Health / Health Connect, named not read); detections carry phase= + phase_basis=stated-rhythm-inferred; per-phase baselines split at >=5 rounds/phase; 2026-11-22 (home sick) filed as the atypical-day worked example. Publication: band profile joins the published entropy stream by procedure (one line; publication pass owns the feed). CRON WIRED: qts-daily-drift-check updated (viewed before and after): listen.py added as step 6 after drift_check; chat reports ONLY on a detection (explained=none is the headline) or a detector failure; schedule (daily 07:00:28 America/Chicago) and MOAT delivery preserved. Stream lengths future detections need: offset-break arms at a node's 4th fresh sample (~first week of daily rounds); handwriting-drift at 5 fresh ppm samples; cadence candidates 14 rounds; theta phase baselines 30; delta tides 30; aging 90. No journal sampling was taken - the atoms sufficed for calibration. Task stays OPEN: the detector is the deliverable and it is standing; the listening continues by cron.
- BRAIN.PHEXT REVISION 1 COMMITTED 2026-11-22 (EE 98): brain.phext + revise_brain.py landed on wbic16/exobrain@exo as commit 5469565b845c0e6294cbef30434ff49457eb9002, parented on the deploy agent's fresh app-tree HEAD c1a24622 (push_tree.py read HEAD fresh at push time, force disabled; no race, nothing overwritten -- the app tree, registers/, publish/, systemd units all coexist). brain.phext: 24 scrolls, 45,842 B, sha256 a85652f33f1d34166ecca5189609b673098215072bd8f94197bd832328d22441 -- read back from the remote and SHA-256-matched; revise_brain.py at mode 100755, hash-matched. Built ONLY via the reference implementation (libphext Python port 0.4.0, ~/workspace/libphext-py-work/port): explode==scroll map and implode(explode)==bytes verified in the generator, and the port first proved conformant on foreign material (wiring.phext 22,737 B / 14 scrolls, explode->implode byte-exact). Structure: home 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1 (provenance, band frame from LISTENING-1, honesty rule, revision rule), fleet aggregate 1.1.1/1.1.2/1.1.1, Will-phase reference layer 1.1.1/1.1.3/1.1.1, 20 node scrolls 1.1.1/1.1.10-29/1.1.1 (fleet.txt order + great-white), revision log 1.1.1/1.2.1/1.1.1. Revision 1 records: alpha OBSERVED per node (3 rounds: fresh ppm/offset sequences, ranges, freshness/stale marks, apply-round values flagged as re-convergence for switched nodes only); beta events where they occurred (4 regime-flips + slew-storm/clamp rounds, explained=qts-apply; jacked-kite/moon-beam/rock-mountain candidates carried as analyst notes); gamma as the census jitter grain per round (quiet-set membership derived mechanically = LISTENING-1's set exactly) with sampling profile UNKNOWN; delta and theta UNKNOWN for every node (needs >=30 / >=14-30 daily rounds; all 3 rounds waking-phase). Aggregate carries consensus/blend/elections/drift state through census EE 94 (managed offset -3.981 ms, drift cross-ref -5.914 ppm within tolerance, master+conduit elven-path) and the detector's calibration state. Revision rule (home scroll): regenerate via revise_brain.py (pure function of QTS registers + prior revision + instant), a new revision on material change (detector/band call) and at minimum on the daily round, one commit per revision to exobrain@exo; the node-resident app runs it after each daily round against its local registers (the deploy shipped registers/ in the repo tree) and commits. Staging: ~/workspace/brain-phext/ (revise_brain.py, brain.phext, push/).
- NI-KNIGHT OFFSET-BREAK CLASSIFIED (2026-11-22 17:42Z): verdict EXPLAINED, explained=qts-triangulation. The round-16 (EE 97) detection - ni-knight offset -97.461 ms vs prior median -0.452 ms, bound 75 ms, explained=none - is the triangulation daemon swap's own footprint. Evidence: the atom (stamp 2026-11-22T17:21:28Z, from the shared register inbox) precedes the on-node swap by 74 s but sits inside the triangulation apply window (events record 17:20:00Z-18:00:00Z) and inside round 16 (assembled 17:26:40Z); on ni-knight, apt-get install chrony ran 17:22:38Z, systemd-timesyncd stopped 17:22:40Z, chrony 4.5 first started 17:22:42Z (dpkg: chrony 4.5-1ubuntu4.2 installed, systemd-timesyncd removed), stock chrony.conf backed up as chrony.conf.qts-bak-20261122T112255Z with final-config restart 17:22:55Z. The reading itself was a single timesyncd sample whose own jitter (156.094 ms) exceeds the excursion. No step and no large-offset correction appears in ni-knight's journal at chrony's first discipline, and post-swap offsets converged: +0.487 ms in the triangulation census EE 99 (2026-11-22T17:32:02Z, engine=chrony, jitter back to 13.280 ms) and +1.4 ms in live chrony tracking at 17:40Z - the clock was never displaced; the excursion was apply-window measurement scatter. Classification line appended to detections.txt in ~/workspace/quantum-time-sync/ and in the shared register (/mnt/mirrorborn/shared/exobrain/detections.txt); a daemon-swap known-work note appended to events.txt in both places (and the fleet triangulation event, present locally, was also appended to the shared events.txt which lacked it), so future detector runs treat daemon swaps as known work.
- CALIBRATION STANCE (Will, 2026-11-22 14:27 CST, on the EE 109 explained=none detections: verbatim 'assume our clocks suck, they're cheap') - the fleet's crystals are cheap hardware; tens-of-ppm wander, thermal/DVFS drift, and slew weather are the NULL MODEL, not anomalies. Detections remain candidates until a falsifier check fails; the burden is on the anomaly claim, not on the cheap-clock explanation. Falsifier checks on the EE 109 set dispatched the same minute (free-fall journal for step/restart; surveyor mesh state).
# Standing rule — 5-minute task-concurrency watchdog (floor of 4, ranch-first)

