Status: open — design: bucket real output by hour of day (git commits + lines changed across his repos, tasks closed, register items resolved) so late-night hours show their true rate against earlier hours; the display's job is to make the case for an earlier bedtime in his own numbers. Serves the primary metrics set 2026-11-14 (healthy, stable, effective, legible) and his yardstick rule (he does best measured against something concrete). Natural home: a panel on the perpetual dashboard. Build while he sleeps; first view ready for the morning.
# phccbox binary slicer (design brief)

Coordinate: 26.5.10/6.5.9/5.5.8
Issued: 2026-11-21 22:44 CST
Request (verbatim): phccbox needs a way to slice programs from binary form — like a disassembler on steroids
Status: in progress

Results:
- DESIGN DELIVERED 2026-11-21. Brief at ~/workspace/goals/finish-phcc/files/phccbox-slicer-design.md; ruling bullet added to the finish-phcc GOAL.md design rulings (phccbox side, per division of custody — commands live in phccbox). Design: lift (container parse + recursive-descent disassembly) → graph (CFG + call graph + def-use dependence graph) → slice (backward/forward by criterion: address+register, or an output point) → emit as phext (every slice a scroll at its own coordinate, provenance = binary offsets; binaries become diffable corpora) → round-trip (a slice re-emitted as a linkable fragment — how the box reads and reuses the binary-only 1978-1990 substrate). Built on phcc's own architecture backends (x86 first), no Capstone/Ghidra chain (human-slop rule); slices differentially verified against the source binary (phcc's provably-correct ethos); deterministic output. First build step: static backward slicer, x86-64 ELF, test corpus = phcc's own compiled samples (source + binary both ours). Open: the component's name, drawn from Singular per the naming rule — not coined in the brief.
