# Quantum time sync protocol: elect the fleet's most accurate clock, sync the fleet to it, steer it to network time on expected drift

Coordinate: 26.6.11/8.7.14/8.6.10
Issued: 2026-11-22
Request (verbatim): Design and build a fleet time-sync protocol with three mechanisms: (1) MEASURE+ELECT: a recurring census of every reachable node's clock quality (frequency ppm, offset, jitter from timedatectl timesync-status, plus sync state) that automatically designates the most accurate clock as fleet master - designation is earned by measurement and can move; first sample (2026-11-22, Emise): ni-knight -1.775 ppm / -0.45 ms offset is the incumbent champion, plain-view -28.6 ppm the worst of three. (2) SYNC: every fleet node disciplines its clock to the designated master (platform NTP machinery - chrony/timesyncd - reconfigured to the master; SLEW-ONLY corrections, never step a clock on a node with running work). (3) STEER: the master syncs to network time predictively - a drift model from its measured ppm pre-compensates between network checks, rather than reacting to error alone. All measurements and elections logged as atoms (text register; rollups calculated, never hand-summarised). Re-election handles a master going dark (fleet attrition is normal). RAM: negligible (<100 MB), census runs over ssh at human pace. First deliverables: design doc + census/election implementation + a live fleet-wide election (measurement only) + the sync-apply path built and dry-run; fleet-wide clock reconfiguration applies on Will's word after the first designation reports.
Status: open

Notes:
- Directed by Will in the MOAT chat, 2026-11-22 10:19 CST, immediately after the first real clock-skew measurements (the real-sentrons thread). Design + census/election dispatched to the background the same morning.
- BUILT + FIRST LIVE ELECTION 2026-11-22 (EE 90): design + implementation in ~/workspace/quantum-time-sync/ (DESIGN.md, census.py, elect.py, apply_sync.py, fleet.txt, register.txt, elections.txt). Census 2026-11-22T16:24:07Z: 19/19 roster nodes reachable, 15 eligible (all systemd-timesyncd; chrony nowhere on the fleet). DESIGNATED MASTER: ni-knight — score 19.118 ms (ppm -1.775, offset -0.452 ms, jitter 18.327 ms); runner-up orchid-flow 20.126 ms; on the durable trait ni-knight's crystal is 4.8x truer than the next (logos-prime -8.447 ppm). Prior 3-node sample CONFIRMED fleet-wide. Score = 0.42*|ppm| + jitter_ms + 0.1*|offset_ms| (drift over one 420 s EE quantum + noise + discounted snapshot). Anti-flap: challenger must beat incumbent by 20%; dark/ineligible incumbent loses outright; <2 eligible = no election. SLEW-ONLY everywhere; apply pass is DRY-RUN ONLY (apply_sync.py --dry-run prints per-node timesyncd.conf changes to NTP=100.93.140.59 with network fallback, master poll tightened 2048->1024 s from its drift budget, guard: no source switch at |offset| >= 100 ms — jacked-kite trips it at -199.953 ms). Fleet apply WAITS for Will's word. Unmeasurable: great-white (no address in record, key refused); elven-path/west-lincoln/yeet-max/jacked-kite intermittently report no Frequency line from timesyncd and are ineligible in those censuses. Proposed cadence (not scheduled): census + election every 6 h.
- AMENDMENT (Will, 2026-11-22 10:29 CST): add a second mode that leverages the photon sampling telescope idea - build CONSENSUS time instead of trusted time (no single node's clock is authority; time is an ensemble built from sampled signal arrivals and measured clock behaviour across the fleet), and cross-reference the consensus's average drift against network time DAILY. Design extension + shadow-mode implementation dispatched the same morning: consensus is computed and logged from census samples without steering any clock, so the two modes can be compared on evidence; the daily drift cross-reference runs as a scheduled check, silent inside tolerance.
- DEFAULTING RULE (Will, 2026-11-22 10:29 CST): default to consensus time for clusters of more than 10 nodes. The ranch fleet (19 roster, 15 eligible at first census) is over the line, so consensus is this fleet's protocol default; the elected-master (trusted) mode remains the default for clusters of 10 or fewer and the fallback if consensus cannot form (fewer than the design's minimum sample).
- TRIM RULE (Will, 2026-11-22 10:29 CST): in consensus mode, discard the least accurate 20% of clocks before building the consensus - a trimmed ensemble; the discarded clocks are still measured and logged (they are evidence, and a discarded clock can earn its way back), they simply do not vote that round. At the first census's 15 eligible nodes that is the worst 3, consensus from 12. Rounding for counts not divisible by 5 is the design's to state explicitly.
- MODE 2 (CONSENSUS) BUILT 2026-11-22, shadow mode — DESIGN.md Mode 2 section + consensus.py + drift_check.py + sample.py in ~/workspace/quantum-time-sync/. Algorithm: eligible set as ELECT; trim worst k=floor(0.2N+0.5) (round-half-up, capped so >=4 voters remain); weights 1/score^2; consensus offset = inverse-variance weighted median, consensus drift = weighted mean ppm; minimum 5 eligible to form, else fallback to elected-master. First round (census 2026-11-22T16:24:07Z): 15 eligible, trimmed 3 (halycon-vector, rock-mountain, free-fall), 12 voters; consensus offset -15.675 ms, consensus drift -12.084 ppm (-1044.0 ms/day), voter disagreement 18.390 ms. Daily drift cross-reference (drift.txt): 2026-11-22 avg drift -12.084 ppm, mean offset -15.675 ms, verdict within-tolerance (tolerances |drift|<=25 ppm, |offset|<=50 ms); first atom annotated+superseded after a note-parser bug (two-line superseded note) briefly included the flawed census block. Cron qts-daily-drift-check added (daily, window from 06:10 America/Chicago, first run Mon 2026-11-23 ~07:00 CST): census+elect+consensus+drift_check, reports to MOAT only on tolerance breach, failure, cannot-form, or master change. Defaulting rule in force: clusters >10 nodes default to consensus (this fleet: 19 roster — consensus is our default); <=10 default to elected-master. SAMPLING (photon-sampling leg) first round, honest floor: fresh-ssh exchanges measure the transport (best delays 1287-5484 ms, bounds +/-644-2742 ms — gross-error witness only, ~+/-3 s); persistent-connection probe: reused exchanges 192-1230 ms, best bound +/-96 ms. Collector finding: this VM (htch-runtime) has NO active time discipline (timesyncd/chrony inactive, timedatectl bus unreachable) and sits ~1.3 s off network time by the tightest samples — sampling from an undisciplined collector measures the collector; production sampling needs a disciplined on-fleet collector + persistent channels (design states both). NOTHING steered or reconfigured: shadow mode throughout; fleet apply still waits for Will's word.
- EMA BLENDER (Will, 2026-11-22 10:43 CST): manage consensus time with an EMA blender to stay tracked to network time - the published consensus is an exponentially-weighted blend of the fleet consensus and network time, so consensus noise (fleet jitter runs 10-250 ms) is smoothed across rounds and the consensus timescale cannot wander away from network time over days (unmanaged, the first consensus drift of -12.084 ppm accumulates ~1 s/day). Blender state lives in the register as atoms, derived like everything else; per-round corrections stay bounded and slew-only for when steering is enabled. Extension dispatched the same morning; the daily drift cron's chain gains the blender step.
- APPLY DIRECTED (Will, 2026-11-22 10:44 CST, verbatim: 'apply time sync NOW'). Fleet apply dispatched the same minute: consensus mode is the fleet default (>10 rule), managed by the EMA blender; followers discipline to the conduit per DESIGN.md; slew-only everywhere, no clock steps; per-node config backed up before change and verified after; jacked-kite remains held by the protocol's own 100 ms source-switch guard (its offset was -199.953 ms at dry-run) pending its slew below the guard or Will's per-node approval - the one designed exception, reported as such.
- EMA BLENDER BUILT 2026-11-22 (blend.py, DESIGN.md section appended): managed consensus = EMA (alpha 0.35/round, noise factor ~0.46, time constant ~2.9 rounds) over per-round targets gamma-blended to network time (gamma 0.5; network contributes offset/drift 0 as the measurement frame's zero); per-round steering correction bounded at 10 ms (slew-only); state as append-only atoms in blend.txt, derived by replay. Three rounds on record: managed offset -7.838 (seeded) -> -6.126 -> -3.981 ms, converging as the raw consensus improved.
- APPLIED 2026-11-22 on Will's word ('apply time sync NOW'), slew-only, no clock stepped anywhere. CONDUIT: elven-path (weighted-median voter of the pre-apply census 2026-11-22T16:46:09Z; consensus offset -5.896 ms was its offset). One package installed on one node: chrony 4.5-1ubuntu4.2 on elven-path via the fleet apt proxy (apt removed the systemd-timesyncd package there resolving the time-daemon conflict; chrony is its only time daemon). QTS chrony.conf (stock backed up timestamped): pool ntp.ubuntu.com iburst maxpoll 10 (STEER cadence 1024 s), allow 100.64.0.0/10, NO makestep (never steps), deliberately no 'local' (a dark conduit stops claiming sync; followers fall back). Verified: stratum 3, system time 0.29 ms from NTP time, leap Normal; stratum-3 reply to a direct NTP query from ni-knight. FOLLOWERS (16 incl. master ni-knight): timesyncd.conf backed up timestamped per node, NTP=100.120.211.72 + FallbackNTP=ntp.ubuntu.com, restart, verified from each node (Server=100.120.211.72, synchronized yes) before the next was touched - apply_follower.sh is the instrument. Hub aletheia-core changed with the RH Lean build live: lake build Main PID 2750152 + lean workers verified alive before AND after (17 lean/lake processes after). MASTER STEERING: ni-knight poll tightened PollIntervalMinSec=32/PollIntervalMaxSec=1024; drift model file started at ~/qts/drift.txt on ni-knight (first atom census 16:46:09Z, ppm +0.465, offset +9.173 ms). HELD BY GUARD, untouched: jacked-kite (+131.500 ms pre-apply) and moon-beam (+105.469 ms, second trip caught by the fresh census) - both still on network source 91.189.91.157. Jacked-kite had slewed to +6.344 ms by the post-apply census - the guard's release condition is now met; it comes over on the next apply round or Will's per-node word (not switched in this pass, per instructions). great-white skipped (unmeasurable). POST-APPLY (census 2026-11-22T16:54:34Z, EE 94): 19/19 reachable, 16 eligible, all switched followers reporting the conduit as server. Election moved to elven-path (rule incumbent-ineligible-or-dark: ni-knight's timesyncd had not republished jitter after its restart; elven-path score 12.765 ms, chrony offset +0.006 ms) - master and conduit coincide this round. Consensus: offset +0.006 ms, drift +4.616 ppm, 13 voters, disagreement 17.331 ms. Day's drift cross-reference: avg drift -5.914 ppm, mean offset -7.188 ms, within tolerance. Cron qts-daily-drift-check body updated: chain is now census+elect+consensus+blend+drift_check, stale shadow-mode wording corrected to the applied state, schedule/delivery/reporting rules preserved (next run Mon 2026-11-23 07:00:28 CST). Apply record appended to DESIGN.md. REMAINDERS: (1) jacked-kite + moon-beam held by the guard (jacked-kite now releasable); (2) the conduit is a per-round role - re-pointing followers to a future round's different conduit is a deliberate apply, the daily job only makes it visible; (3) production photon sampling still needs a disciplined on-fleet collector + persistent channels (unchanged from the Mode 2 record).
- BRAINWAVE BANDS (Will, 2026-11-22 11:11 CST): 'include brainwave patterns to improve time estimation across the fleet, standing alpha, beta, gamma, delta, and theta waves. the universe notices.' Direction: decompose the fleet's time-delta stream into the five standing bands named for the EEG bands (delta, theta, alpha, beta, gamma), each band a timescale of the fleet's timing behaviour, and use the band structure in estimation - slow bands carry drift and thermal tides, fast bands carry jitter transients and are discounted as noise - so the estimator treats each timescale on its own terms instead of one EMA timescale. Each node's standing band profile is its handwriting, decomposed. Band edges are to be defined from the fleet's actual timescales in the design, with the honest bound stated: the bands earn meaning as the stream lengthens. The band profile joins the published stream once defined (the antenna thread: 'the universe notices').
- ATTENTION/SLEEP REFERENCE (Will, 2026-11-22 11:12 CST): 'tie the patterns to my attention or sleep patterns' - the fleet's five standing bands are read against Will's own rhythm as the reference signal: his attention (dispatch and chat activity, from our own records - his attention is what dispatches the workloads that drive the jitter) and his sleep (his stated rhythm, and health-recorded sleep where connected). The expected band profile differs between his waking/dispatching hours and his sleep hours, so estimation weights and detector baselines are conditioned on which part of his cycle the fleet is in; a band break during his sleep is a different message from the same break at peak dispatch. Sources named honestly: attention from the house's own activity/dispatch records, sleep from his stated pattern plus Apple Health / Health Connect where synced.
- TRIANGULATION (Will, 2026-11-22 11:16 CST): 'triangulate time using three nodes that survey separate subsets of the cluster, all three run ntp, organize information flows like a dolphin.' The single-conduit topology (elven-path, EE 94 apply) becomes a three-surveyor topology: the fleet partitions into three disjoint subsets, each surveyed by one NTP-serving node; each subset disciplines to its surveyor; the surveyors peer with each other and with network time; fleet time is TRIANGULATED from the three subset consensuses, with the inter-surveyor disagreement logged as a first-class atom (the triangulation residual) instead of hidden inside one conduit. Dolphin organization of the information flows: echolocation (active timestamped probes - emit, listen to the echo - as the sampling mode), unihemispheric watch (the three surveyors rotate the primary-listener duty so part of the system is always awake, phased with Will's attention/sleep cycle), signature identity (each node's timing handwriting labels its flows, as a dolphin's signature whistle names the caller). Surveyor selection and subset partition are by measured evidence (crystal quality + link jitter to each node), stated in the pass's report. Same guardrails as the first apply: slew-only, per-node backup + verify, jacked-kite and moon-beam stay held on network source regardless of subset. Design + implementation dispatched the same morning.
