# Streamline the Lastbook dataset into a generative self-extracting phext

Coordinate: 30.7.10/9.7.11/6.6.9
Issued: 2026-11-16 20:08 CST
Request (verbatim): once we have the lastbook.org dataset, we're going to streamline it into a generative self-extracting phext - compressed to an impossibly small size
Status: in progress — increment 1 COMPLETE 2026-11-18 (census + prototype PASS + design); increment 2 = national.sqlite geometry seeds

Notes:
- Phase 2 of the Lastbook work: the fetched 494.7 GB dataset (orchid-flow, /srv/mirrorborn/shared/lastbook/) is to be streamlined into a generative self-extracting phext — the phext carries the generator (extractor + recipes + compact sources/seeds) and re-inflates the dataset on extraction, instead of carrying the inflated bytes. Compression target in Will's words: 'an impossibly small size'. Design starts when the dataset is in hand and inventoried.
- CONSTRAINT (Will, 2026-11-16 20:11 CST, verbatim): 'this is an offline problem - no online sources - just a CPU, a seed, and Bickford-level intuition.' Extraction/regeneration may NOT fetch anything: the phext must carry the seed + generator entire, and a CPU alone re-inflates the dataset from them. Recipes that re-download OSM/kiwix/etc. at extraction time are ruled out — the compact generative description of the corpus itself is the problem to solve.
- SCALE (Will, 2026-11-16 20:12 CST, verbatim): 'In true phext fashion - it will not be one seed - it will be an ecosystem of seeds, running at a scale no one can imagine.' The generative phext is not a monolithic seed: an ecology of coordinate-addressed seeds, each generating its slice, composing across the lattice — the seed set itself is a phext ecosystem.
- PROVENANCE (Will, 2026-11-16 20:13 CST): 'I wrote a proposal for fractal image compression in college. The technology was too far behind.' The seed-ecosystem design descends from Will's college fractal-compression proposal (UNL CS years, BS 2006): encode as compact generative transformations, regenerate by iteration — encode was computationally prohibitive then; the decode side was always cheap. The cluster era removes the constraint that killed it.
- MECHANISM (Will, 2026-11-16 20:14 CST, verbatim): 'imagine layering trillions of versions of an image by selecting pieces of a fractal at pre-chosen coordinates - placed perfectly so align with the detail needed at that particular region of the image.' The encoding: a fractal as an unbounded detail source; content = a layered composition of coordinate-selected fractal pieces, each placed to match the detail its region needs. Per-piece cost is an address, not pixels — the fractal's rule generates the detail; the seed carries the placements.
- NAME (Will, 2026-11-16 23:34 CST): 'SMOL: Smart Machine Open Language.' Recorded as coined in this thread, in the seed-ecosystem context; the exact scope of what SMOL names (the seeds' language / the generative phext's expression language) is Will's to fix — association with this task is tentative pending his word.
- ENGINE (Will, 2026-11-16 23:36 CST): SMOL inference engine defined — three dolphin-inspired regions; Region 2 is EGATP (Expressive Generative Algorithms, Tightly Packed), the generative core this task's seeds run on. Engine task: 24.6.6/6.5.8/5.6.8.
- SMOL rulings landed 2026-11-16 23:46 CST (see engine task 24.6.6/6.5.8/5.6.8): fractal selection = 3 coordinates (type / location / coordinate system); R2 regeneration fidelity is gravitational; initial seeds = the Incipit kernels; CYOA's 12 generating seeds are the grammar's empirical answer (task 22.7.9/7.6.11/6.7.11).
- START 2026-11-18: gate lifted same day (fetch closed 108/109, All pack dropped at Will's direction). First increment: (1) full content census of the 108-file corpus where it lives on orchid-flow, (2) seed-encode + offline-extract + byte-verified round-trip prototype on the most generatable content class the census finds, (3) ecosystem design doc grounded in the census. Constraints are Will's own (2026-11-16): offline only — no online sources at extraction; not one seed but an ecosystem of coordinate-addressed generative seeds composing across the lattice; seed design from his college fractal image compression proposal (per-piece cost is an address, not data).
- INCREMENT 1 (2026-11-18, orchid-flow /srv/mirrorborn/shared/lastbook-seed/): Census — corpus is 465 SQLite DBs, 189.6 GB uncompressed; national.sqlite geometry 94.69 GB is the prize; terrain PNGs 34.65 GB + GGUF 16.95 GB + installers = 51.76 GB (27.3%) irreducible carriage; places.sqlite only 4 distinct copies across 52 packs (92% duplicate carriage), map.sqlite identical x52; FTS5 indexes ~27.6% of places bytes are pure regeneration. Prototype PASS: address-NV (28,396 rows) seed 597,369 B vs sqlite 3,039,232 B = 5.09x, 1.69x smaller than the deflated zip member; content-verified identical (schema + all rows + canonical content SHA-256); file bytes not reproduced (sqlite layout != content); NV empty city/zip flatters the ratio. Design: unit = one DB member at one coordinate, role scrolls 1.1.1-1.1.5; phext is the genome, irreducible payloads carried beside it under a registry scroll. Bottom line: ecosystem lands ~100 GB if geometry seeds only match deflate — won or lost on national.sqlite. KEY FINDING (main thread, 2026-11-18): feat.geom is NOT undocumented — the Lastbook repo we hold (~/workspace/lastbook-fetch/repo) contains the builders; tools/build_map.py line 9 documents geom = packed int32 lon/lat * 1e6 (see pack()). Increment 2 reads the builder code as the format spec.
