# Re-run Holostore integration: eliminate complexity — phext-native re-implementation + 4-way comparison

Coordinate: 30.8.11/10.8.13/8.8.13
Issued: 2026-11-15
Request (verbatim): Re-implement Holostore's core (durable ordered writes, latest+versioned reads, crash recovery) phext-natively in the wbic16/holostore fork, as simply as the format allows. Compare against the original on performance, development cost, stability, legibility. Record every phext feature found missing or illegible during the build. No upstream merge needed.
Status: complete

Notes:
- Standing directive (Will, 2026-11-15): integration work optimizes for testing the complexity-elimination hypothesis and for finding missing/illegible phext features, not for upstream mergeability or time cost.
- Standing rule (Will, 2026-11-15): eliminate Hull hedging; think only from first principles. Saved to MEMORY.md.

Results:
- Built crates/holo_phext in wbic16/holostore (branch emise/phext-analysis, commits a8f5424 + 59c1456; PR #1 https://github.com/wbic16/holostore/pull/1): 717-line single-file phext-native storage core — one phext file = manifest scroll + snapshot volume + WAL volume; group-commit appends (delimiter run + text, fdatasync per batch); recovery via libphext with torn-tail truncation; checkpoint via implode rewrite; RESP surface (PING/SET/GET/DEL/EXISTS/DBSIZE/INFO/CHECKPOINT). 4/4 unit tests pass (codec, 10,050-append libphext conformance across a chapter boundary, checkpoint fold, torn tail). Independent Python phext parser reads the store file exactly (1,001 scrolls).
- Performance (aletheia-core, release, same Python RESP client both servers): SET pipeline-50 152,471 ops/s vs cluster 203,792; GET pipeline-50 341,613 vs 76,168; SET pipeline-1 9,837 vs 5,684; GET pipeline-1 (50 thr) 51,924 vs 6,653. Asymmetry stated in the report: the cluster pays Accord consensus + RF3; the re-implementation is single-node — the comparison isolates the persistence/serving layer phext claims to simplify.
- Stability: SIGKILL mid-load (2M-key run): 272,500 acked at kill -> 272,500 present after restart, 0 lost; DBSIZE 273,000 (one in-flight synced batch beyond acks). Earlier v1 run: 17,450 acked, 0 lost. Torn tail truncated, store keeps working. Checkpoint folds 350k keys (16.0 MB -> 12.1 MB), restart-verified. Bugs found by measurement and fixed: checkpoint dir-sync on bare relative paths; Nagle stalls (GET 12k -> 499k); per-connection reply serialization (writes 11.9k -> 152k).
- Development cost: 717 lines / 1 direct dependency (3 crates in tree) / one session, vs the original storage core's 12,331 lines (wal+kv+cluster+redis_server+packed_batch) / 33 direct deps (~900 crates in tree). Legibility: one text file (head shows manifest + key=value scrolls) vs raft-engine binaries + Fjall SSTs + two JSON sidecars + epoch file.
- Six missing/illegible phext features recorded in PHEXT.md round 2: (1) no append/streaming writer in libphext; (2) no per-scroll integrity (whole-phext seal only) — torn writes detected by a trailing-newline convention; (3) no atomic multi-scroll commit primitive; (4) no documented durability contract for phext hosts; (5) empty-scroll addressing friction at volume/book boundaries (WAL addressing made relative to snapshot baseline to route around it); (6) binary values don't compose inside line-structured scrolls (base64, +33%).
- PHEXT.md round 2 (401 lines, sha256 789b3562...) pushed to the fork; inscription refreshed at artifacts.phext 34.7.7/8.6.11/7.6.9, artifacts.phext pushed to human@exo commit 062d1513, remote sha256 verified identical (2dd3c995...).
