# Cross-reference: RFCs conflicting with phext's re-assignment of C0 control codes

Coordinate: 34.7.7/9.6.12/6.6.9
Issued: 2026-11-18
Request (verbatim): Will (2026-11-18 20:11 CST): 'cross-reference RFCs that conflict with the re-assignment of control codes to phext use' — phext (libphext-rs v0.6.0, per draft-bickford-phext-00) assigns structural roles to C0 bytes: 0x01 Library, 0x1F Shelf, 0x1E Series, 0x1D Collection, 0x1C Volume, 0x1A Book, 0x19 Chapter, 0x18 Section, 0x17 Scroll, 0x02 chain break, 0x03 chain close, 0x07 detection (MORE_COWBELL). Produce the cross-reference of IETF RFCs whose standardized assignments or text rules conflict with those uses, each verified against the RFC's own text with section cites, classified by conflict kind and consequence.
Status: COMPLETE — XREF.md delivered (16 RFCs, all claims verified against RFC texts with section cites).

Notes:
- RESULT (2026-11-18): ~/workspace/phext-rfc/XREF.md. 16 RFCs examined — Class A competing assignment: 3 (RFC 20 root; RFC 7464; RFC 854 single-byte). Class B prohibition faces: 10 (5198, 8259, 5321, 5322, 2045, 5545 + restrictive faces of 9110/9112/959/3986). Class C coexistence faces: 7 (2849, 3629, 5234 + permissive faces: HTTP content is *OCTET; FTP image type; phext's coordinate-in-URL form is URI-clean — digits/./slash/; only). MOST DIRECT CONFLICT: RFC 7464 assigns 0x1E (RS) as the record-prefix delimiter of application/json-seq; phext assigns 0x1E to Series break — the two systems silently mis-segment each other's streams, and media-type labeling is the only separator (phext's lack of a registered media type = the load-bearing gap). SHARP FINDING in RFC 20 S5.2: the four information separators are standardized 'FS most inclusive ... US least inclusive'; phext's order among them (US Shelf > RS Series > GS Collection > FS Volume) is EXACTLY INVERTED. Also: 0x18 CAN ('data ... is in error or is to be disregarded') is phext's Section break. SURPRISE NON-CONFLICT: RFC 2849 (LDIF) SAFE-CHAR admits every phext structural byte verbatim — it excludes exactly NUL/LF/CR, the three bytes phext also doesn't use structurally. Synthesis: for every closed context the sanctioned traversal answer is the base64 armoring libphext-rs v0.6.0 already ships (binary scrolls); NOTHING surveyed requires phext to change an assignment — the conflicts define its border, not a defect in its interior.
