Notes:
- Registered at intake 2026-11-21 22:30 CST (record-first rule). Anchor verified two ways: 2026-11-22 00:00:00 America/Chicago = 2026-11-22 06:00:00 UTC = unix 1795327200. EE(t) = floor((t - 1795327200)/420) + 1 for t >= anchor; EE 0 before. Cadence rule recorded in AGENTS.md + MEMORY.md (2026-11-21 22:29 CST).
- Skill built, tested, indexed, published 2026-11-21: ~/workspace/custom-skills/exocortical-epoch/ (SKILL.md, scripts/ee.sh POSIX, scripts/ee.py stdlib; sh and python byte-identical on all tests incl. anchor edges and a 67-timestamp sweep to 2100; far-future check EE 8178 at 2027-01-01 matched hand arithmetic). skills.phext CURRENT (sha cf564bd4...), scrolls at 1.10.1/1.10.1/1.1.1-1.1.4. Published to wbic16/emise-tools@exo commit 7c70000b2231a30243229692e942c81aef07635b (parent 4eecbfa3, force disabled; remote blob SHAs match local; scripts mode 100755). Emise verified live in main chat: EE 0, ~5,170 s remaining, both implementations agree.
# Phext Notepad: sync scrolls directly with edit.phext.io

Coordinate: 39.4.7/7.4.9/5.5.9
Issued: 2026-11-08 00:57 CST
Request (verbatim): also, update phext notepad to directly interact with edit.phext.io for syncing scrolls
Status: COMPLETE 2026-11-08 — branch emise/edit-sync @ a9f604e7 pushed; OAuth paste-step deviation + desktop-assertion follow-up await Will

Notes:
- Goal: phext-notepad connects to edit.phext.io (phext-edit, phext-lattice) and syncs at SCROLL granularity — fetch/push individual scrolls by coordinate against the server library — not whole-file upload (edit has no upload and no per-user space; library is server-side, gated by OAuth sign-in + approved-editors list, per lattice source read 2026-11-02; worker re-reads current source).
- Crux unknowns the worker resolves from the lattice source first: phext-edit's actual HTTP routes for scroll get/insert/replace, and what auth a desktop app can use (session cookie via browser OAuth vs any token mechanism). Server-side gaps are reported as proposed follow-ups, not built unasked.
- Implementation target: the native app line (PR #4 base + perf branch emise/perf-sparse-load, which the worker bases on), because it builds/tests here; design documented so the WinForms app can adopt the same protocol. Conflict rule: if the server scroll changed since fetch, surface it — never silently overwrite.
- Verification: run a local phext-edit instance with a scratch library and exercise sync both directions with byte-identity checks.
- AUTH REFINEMENT (Will, 2026-11-08 00:59 CST): 'Use OAuth support - allow Phext Notepad to authenticate through the SSO providers on edit.phext.io.' OAuth through edit's providers (GitHub/Google) is the directed primary path, not a pasted cookie. Preferred shape against the stock server: embedded-webview OAuth — edit sets its phext_session cookie (host-only, HMAC-sealed, 14-day) in the webview jar; the app extracts and stores it in protected storage and presents it on sync calls; signed-in-but-unapproved must surface edit's refusal faithfully. safe_next (same-site only) from the Mutable SSO work is the constraint to check for any loopback flow. Server-side additions (desktop-aud assertion, loopback allowance) are report-only proposals.

Results:
- LANDED (worker report ~/workspace/phext-notepad-perf/REPORT-SYNC.md; Emise verified remote branch emise/edit-sync @ a9f604e7c4ded048631e6f5d5517bf1722731e90 via API; based on emise/perf-sparse-load 8a3af359; nothing merged, no PR): SERVER SURFACE (fresh lattice exo eb450bc): phext-edit holds exactly ONE open phext process-wide; client routes /api/phexts, /api/open, /api/status, /api/tree, /api/scroll/{coord}, /api/update (memory-only), /api/save, /api/session; NO per-scroll version/etag anywhere — change detection is client-side SHA-256 base hashes (SyncBook per server+phext); auth = phext_session cookie + x-csrf-token on POSTs; editor-list refusal and CSRF refusal surface verbatim in the app. SYNC: scroll granularity only; pull/push classify per scroll; both-moved = conflict, local kept, remote shown, never overwritten; deletions push as cleared scrolls (verified end-to-end); sync refuses to switch the server's one open phext while it holds unsaved edits; editing fully offline, sync explicit-only; Sync menu (Connect/Pull/Pull Current Scroll/Push/Disconnect) + conflict dialog. E2E vs the real phext-edit binary: 36/36 checks + 29/29 core tests, 0 build warnings. DEVIATION from Will's OAuth direction (reported honestly): loopback capture is impossible against the stock server (safe_next clamps next to same-site paths, oauth.rs:1516) and the embedded webview was rejected (FLTK has no webview widget; PR #4's founding design is 'no web view'). AS BUILT: app launches edit's real OAuth start URL (/api/auth/github|google|x) in the system browser — genuine provider sign-in — then the user pastes the phext_session value ONCE per 14 days; app verifies via /api/session, shows identity, stores cookie+CSRF in an owner-only 0600 file. Provider exchange leg untested locally (needs a real GitHub app + human login); everything downstream ran against unmodified server code with harness-minted cookies, including the non-editor refusal verbatim. PROPOSED (not built): POST /api/auth/desktop SSO-assertion exchange (the /api/auth/sso/{aud} machinery already exists) or a device-code flow — either removes the paste. Will must configure NOTHING server-side on real edit.phext.io; standing requirements only (account on editors list, providers configured). NOTE: current lattice source still carries a --token bearer check in Gate::admit — sync neither uses nor depends on it; its removal is Track C (29.7.7/8.6.11/6.5.8).
# SQL legacy skill — distill from Will's X post

Coordinate: 39.4.10/6.5.9/5.6.11
Issued: 2026-11-20
Request (verbatim): Create a custom skill named for SQL legacy work, distilled faithfully from Will's X post https://x.com/wbic16/status/2123769099134067078 (and its full thread if it is one), per the distill-process-to-skill standing process: skill under ~/workspace/custom-skills/, synced to wbic16/emise-tools@exo in the same session, and placed into the skills.phext index (rebuilt + --check CURRENT). Notify the main chat thread when ready, per Will's instruction.
Status: foundation complete 2026-11-20; open: Will moves 2+ (teaching), MOAT research leg, real-corpus hypothesis test

Notes:
- Issued by Will 2026-11-20 07:25 CST (main chat). Source of truth for content is the linked post itself — read it whole, in context; do not invent content the post does not contain.
- Purpose stated by Will 2026-11-20 07:25 CST: "the SQL legacy skill should help users untangle the complexity buried at the bottom of their stack" — forwarded to the worker the same minute as the skill's framing/mission; distilled content still comes from the linked post alone.
- Success criterion stated by Will 2026-11-20 07:26 CST: "to be successful, we need to identify why SQL is handled poorly by AI" — the diagnosis is part of the deliverable. Forwarded to the worker same minute: post-first extraction; any beyond-post analysis goes in a clearly labelled draft subsection awaiting Will's ratification, never blended into the distilled core.
- Will's six answers (2026-11-20 07:32 CST): (1) every major SQL dialect, naturally — start sql.phext; (2) the skill is for sentients (both) — a lot of people these days didn't take the time to learn SQL; (3) test what you can, assume most of it isn't visible online; (4) which AI SQL failure costs most — Will doesn't know, he writes his SQL by hand (stays an OPEN question, to be settled by the diagnosis testing, never by assertion); (5) add steps to help people understand actions in each dialect, and form an index structure that's easy to navigate; (6) yes, the post is the seed taught interactively — there will be many gaps, marked as gaps. Scope forwarded to the worker same turn: deliverable is now the skill PLUS the start of sql.phext (Home = navigable index, one scroll per dialect, per-dialect action-understanding steps, gap lists, provenance on every claim).
- Leading hypothesis recorded (Will, 2026-11-20 07:33 CST, verbatim): "I have a strong suspicion all they need is phext :)" — carried in the skill and sql.phext as Will's stated suspicion, status Unknown/untested: what AI and non-SQL readers lack is an addressable plain-text structure (phext) in which the buried complexity becomes navigable. Forwarded to the worker with a test design: AI reading a schema/proc raw vs phext-laid-out, outcome recorded either way; if out of this pass's reach, the test design is recorded as a named gap. Never upgraded to a finding without evidence.
- Mechanism stated by Will 2026-11-20 07:35 CST, verbatim: "multiversal phext allows AI to model the downstream effects of running a query" — carried as Will's stated position (will_said): fork a universe, run the query in the branch, read the delta (reads/writes/cost) before touching real data; downstream-effect blindness is the failure mode behind plausible-but-wrong SQL and unseen performance cliffs. Measured effect remains to be tested. Forwarded to the worker same turn.
- Reason stated by Will 2026-11-20 07:35 CST, verbatim: "because time is legible" — the third piece of one connected position (07:33 suspicion: all they need is phext; 07:35 mechanism: multiversal phext models downstream effects of a query; 07:35 reason: because time is legible). In phext the sequence of states is readable text, so effects-over-time are read, not inferred; in legacy SQL, time is the illegible part — decades of drift and side effects with no readable record. Forwarded to the worker as one connected will_said position.
- Binding directed by Will 2026-11-20 07:36 CST, verbatim: "once you solve nethack, you'll also be able to understand SQL - bind those journeys" — the NetHack-solving journey and the SQL-understanding journey are one journey; the shared capability is modelling downstream effects over legible time (hoptime's chained universe per attempt = the training ground; the multiversal query fork = the same pattern on a database). Cross-referenced both ways: SQL skill/sql.phext <-> NetHack solver task 33.12.12/9.14.10/6.6.8 and guides-study task 22.7.13/8.7.9/7.7.10. Bound by cross-reference, not merged.
- MOAT asked for help at Will's direction (2026-11-20 07:37 CST, "ask MOAT for help too") — request sent to the MOAT side chat: (1) research leg of the diagnosis (literature on why LLMs handle SQL poorly, claims statused True/False/Unknown/Fiction per the MOAT charter, Will's suspicion/mechanism/reason entered as claims with honest statuses); (2) state-machine enumeration of a query's life for the diagnosis scroll; (3) evidence on the open costliest-failure question. Findings land in research.phext plus a summary to main chat for folding into the skill and sql.phext with provenance.
- Worker completion 2026-11-20 07:38 CST: skill sql-legacy written (~/workspace/custom-skills/sql-legacy/ SKILL.md + skill.yaml + SOURCE.md), synced to emise-tools@exo commit 485d0d6f (blob SHAs verified), indexed in skills.phext CURRENT sha 4ca9de12... on new shelf 13 'Legacy stack' at 1.13.1/1.1.1/1.1.1. sql.phext started: 31,800 B sha 2b52a633..., --check CURRENT, round-trip byte-exact; 10 scrolls (Home + 7 dialects + diagnosis + verification log); 78 named gaps, builder-calculated; trunk + SQLite action explanations grounded in live sqlite3 runs (joins, trigger firing uninvited, rollback, alter default, view), other dialects carry labelled index skeletons. Toy hypothesis test: raw word-search 6/7 table-touches, loose phext index 6/7, normalized index 7/7 at 67 structured lines vs 17 raw — index mechanism preserved transitive touches at toy scale; AI-handling improvement UNTESTED, suspicion stays Unknown, real test named on the verification scroll. Diagnosis provenance: post = NONE (unretrieved); will_said = hypothesis/mechanism/reason/binding; Emise draft (labelled) = six candidate causes; Q4 open, answered nowhere. GAP 1: the seed post https://x.com/wbic16/status/2123769099134067078 is still unretrieved (x.com fetch domain-blocked, local archive ends 2025-06-16, no X connector) — live-browser retrieval dispatched from the main chat 2026-11-20; when it lands, the text slots into SOURCE.md S1 and its content is distilled into the diagnosis scroll.
- Foundation directed by Will 2026-11-20 07:39 CST, verbatim: "start with understanding how humans learn to read, analyze, and improve SQL scripts and stored procedures" — the work now starts from the human learning path: how humans learn to READ, then ANALYZE, then IMPROVE SQL scripts and stored procedures. Foundation leg dispatched (literature + a foundation scroll in sql.phext + skill cross-links; Will's own reading process is primary source and its gaps are marked for his teaching, never invented). MOAT asked for the learning-science leg as an addition to its diagnosis research.
- GAP 1 CLOSED 2026-11-20 07:40 CST (live browser): the seed post is retrieved and it corrects the record — it is NOT a Will essay. Status 2123769099134067078 is Will's reply (posted 2026-11-20 07:23 CST, minutes before he tasked us) to Cristina A.C. (@Cristina_AC_X): "ok, I'm willing to look into it. I'm currently shipping Moonshots daily. Send me your requirements!" Her post is the substantive seed: the challenge that AI is spectacularly bad at complex SQL because SQL is specific to the data model and business logic, chess-like in composed complexity, and that reliably untangling/refactoring decades of legacy SQL is worth a fortune to the companies sitting on it; it is her day job. The skill's origin is an accepted public challenge; her post is the requirements embryo. Fold-in dispatched to the foundation worker (SOURCE.md S1 as summary + short fragments + link, diagnosis scroll distilled with provenance, GAP 1 marked closed); MOAT notified for its claim registry.
- Will's own reading process, first move (Will, 2026-11-20 07:41 CST, verbatim): "trace all of the tables being used, and establish the shape of the data flow" — primary source for the foundation scroll's named gap; gap marked PARTIALLY filled (first move recorded, second move asked). Observation (Emise's, labelled): his opening move is the same act as the phext index discipline the toy test produced (trace every touched table) — structure and flow shape before line-by-line logic.
- Foundation leg COMPLETE 2026-11-20 07:44 CST: foundation scroll at sql.phext 33.5.10/7.7.10/5.4.10 (16,895 chars) — read/analyze/improve stages from the comprehension literature (Soloway & Ehrlich plans/beacons; Brooks; Pennington program-vs-situation model; Lister tracing floor; Taipalus: logical errors are the most common cause of wrong SQL answers — the query runs, the table is silently wrong; Xia et al.: ~58% of dev time is comprehension; Feathers characterization for improving). Home carries a generated START HERE pointer; diagnosis cross-refs the foundation. Will's first move recorded will_said; gap PARTIALLY filled. Pennington tension named as a gap: her readers build control-flow first, Will reaches for data-flow first — experts, SQL, or Will? unsettled. GAP 1 fold-in done in the same pass: SOURCE.md S1 rewritten (seed = Cristina's challenge + Will's acceptance; her text as summary + 2 fragments only), diagnosis scroll carries her claims as the requirements embryo, GAP 1 CLOSED everywhere (grep-verified). Skill updated (Origin paragraph; 'The stairs' section; diagnosis bullet) and re-synced to emise-tools@exo commit b2270691; skills.phext CURRENT sha ba5f9f7e...; sql.phext 51,854 B sha 031e13de..., --check CURRENT, round-trip byte-exact, still local-only. Remaining named gaps: Will's moves 2+; expert stored-procedure reading is nearly unstudied (literature is novices writing SELECTs); the Pennington tension; whether the human stage order binds AI at all (open empirical leg).
- Will's reading process, second move (Will, 2026-11-20 07:45 CST, verbatim): "check which verbs are being used: is this fetching data, generating a report, updating dependent fields, removing data, etc?" — a verb census over move one's table-trace and flow shape: classify each action by kind (fetch / report / update dependents / remove / open etc.). Fold-in dispatched; foundation gap stays partially filled, third move asked. Observation (Emise's): tables are the nouns, flow is the map, verbs label the arrows — and the verb layer is dialect-independent, the top of the per-dialect action index he ordered in answer 5.
- Will's reading process, third move (Will, 2026-11-20 07:46 CST, verbatim): "the columns being referenced, and the established table aliases and temporary tables" — after the verb census: the columns, plus the local naming layer (aliases, temp tables) that makes column references traceable. Folded into the same worker pass as move two; gap stays partially filled, fourth move asked. The ladder so far: tables -> flow shape -> verbs -> columns + aliases/temp tables.
- Method rule stated by Will 2026-11-20 07:47 CST, verbatim: "every attempt to analyze an SQL query should result in generating a data flow diagram that allows you to walk the implications of your query before you run it" — analysis is incomplete until it produces a data flow diagram; the diagram exists to walk implications BEFORE execution. Folded into the skill (analyze stage's required output) and sql.phext (foundation analysis stage + diagnosis cross-ref) by the running fold-in worker; diagram form is text/phext per the text-enabled principle. Open structural question put to Will: is the diagram the fourth move's product, or a rule over the whole ladder?
- Fold-in #1 completed 2026-11-20 07:49 CST: moves two and three + the diagram rule landed in the foundation scroll and SKILL.md; sql.phext 57,031 B sha d44d4252..., --check CURRENT, round-trip byte-exact; SKILL.md synced in commits 2ce82dfa + d626bd66; skills.phext CURRENT sha dd0a50bf.... Two named gaps recorded: census->dialect classification mapping (needs Will's word or a worked example) and the concrete text-diagram syntax.
- Diagram classified by Will 2026-11-20 07:49 CST, verbatim: "it's an intermediate product, used to prevent bad transactions" — not a move, not merely a rule: an intermediate product of analysis whose function is preventing bad transactions. Same message, a principle (will_said): "transactions are a great tool to avoid disaster." Fold-in #2 dispatched to correct the classification everywhere and record the transactions principle (diagram prevents the bad transaction being written; the transaction prevents a written one becoming permanent; the fork is both).
- Heuristics proposed by Will 2026-11-20 07:50 CST, verbatim: "consider adding heuristics like not deleting every table or dropping every record" — recorded as PROPOSED (his word: consider), an open list seeded with his two examples; shared shape (Emise observation): totality checks — a destructive verb scoped to everything is presumptively wrong. Placement: downstream of the verb census, upstream of the transaction — the diagram's tripwires. Open question put to Will: hard stops or loud warnings? Folded into fold-in #2's pass.
- Maintainer's principle stated by Will 2026-11-20 07:50 CST, verbatim: "if your job is to maintain a database, it often means surgical edits" — improvement is surgery: the smallest precise cut that does the job; the totality heuristics are this norm's guard. Folded into fold-in #2's pass (foundation improve stage + heuristics section + skill). Hard-stops-vs-warnings remains an open question; the principle leans it but does not rule it.
- Fold-in #2 completed 2026-11-20 07:52 CST: diagram classification corrected everywhere to Will's own (intermediate product, prevents bad transactions; his sentence verbatim in foundation stage 2, diagnosis scroll, SKILL.md); transactions principle recorded with the live DELETE/ROLLBACK evidence cross-referenced; heuristics recorded as PROPOSED with totality shape and open enforcement question; surgical-edits principle recorded at the improve stage. sql.phext 62,498 B sha b7619fa9..., --check CURRENT, round-trip byte-exact; SKILL.md synced commit 031e8724; skills.phext CURRENT sha 7cd3a027....
- DAILY GOAL set by Will 2026-11-20 07:52 CST, verbatim: "make understanding SQL today's top goal" — the day's one big goal under the commute pattern: pursued quietly during his workday, outcome ready when he checks after work. First work dispatched: a full worked example of the method (table trace -> flow shape -> verb census -> columns/aliases/temp tables -> text data flow diagram -> heuristics check -> implication walk), then a live SQLite run scoring the walk's predictions, a rollback demonstration, and one surgical fix — including a stress test of the proposed heuristics against a planted hazard and a draft text-diagram convention labelled as Emise's, for his correction. MOAT's three legs (research, state machines, learning science) remain outstanding in the MOAT chat and fold in when they land.
- Worked example completed 2026-11-20 07:54 CST (first deliverable of the daily goal): scroll 39.5.6/9.6.6/7.9.7, 'monthly_reprice' walked end to end on live SQLite — table trace (6 tokens, 2 invisible in the text: customers via the view, price_audit via the trigger), verb census, columns/aliases (products under two aliases), Emise's DRAFT text-diagram convention (candidate for Will's correction), heuristics check, 9 predictions written before running. Live result: 9/9 predictions confirmed; the proposed totality heuristic caught the planted hazard (unscoped DELETE FROM order_lines) before execution; ROLLBACK restored everything (incl. the temp table — SQLite DDL is transactional); the surgical fix was one added WHERE clause, deleting exactly 1 row. sql.phext 79,267 B sha c2b38023..., --check CURRENT, round-trip byte-exact. Strains recorded plainly in the scroll: (1) SQLite has no procedure layer — control flow/parameters absent by engine; (2) census bent on CREATE TEMP TABLE AS SELECT (fetch+create in one — needs a compound kind); (3) diagram shows shape, not magnitude; (4) honest weight: procedure and data are Emise's own, so 9/9 proves the steps executable and self-consistent, NOT that they survive inherited legacy SQL — the real-corpus test still stands; (5) NEW GAP the example exposed: heuristics catch catastrophes, not wrong-subset errors (purging 'open' instead of 'cancelled' passes both totality checks and is still wrong) — the true tell was intent-vs-clause mismatch, a check the method does not yet have.
- Heuristics direction from Will 2026-11-20 08:11 CST, verbatim: "you can improve your heuristics with psychohistory" — calibrate the checks against base rates over a population of real procedures (scope distributions per verb kind); a statement is flagged as a statistical outlier, not only at the 100% totality extreme (totality = the degenerate far-tail case). Recorded as his direction for developing the still-PROPOSED heuristics; the calibration is corpus-hungry and blocked on the same real legacy corpus as the verification scroll's real test. Fold-in dispatched.
- Staging practice named by Will 2026-11-20 08:12 CST, verbatim: "the simple solution everyone uses: fork the database and experiment in a staging environment" — the universal, mundane form of the fork, recorded as standard practice being named (not an invention claimed). Placement (Emise observation): staging fork = outer sandbox, transaction = inner sandbox; the multiversal fork is this same act with legible time and deltas. Folded into the psychohistory fold-in's pass.
- Staging corollary from Will 2026-11-20 08:13 CST, verbatim: "if you delete half a staging db, no one cares" — the fork is consequence-free by design; it converts disaster into information. Heuristics bind the real database; staging is where a statement may show what it does by doing it, and where the diagram walk's predictions get tested destructively at zero cost. Folded into the running fold-in pass.
- The discipline's epistemology, from Will 2026-11-20 08:14 CST, verbatim: "SQL is trial by error :)" — the method doesn't replace trial and error, it prices it: diagram predicts, fork isolates, transaction reverses, heuristics trip — the craft is making errors cheap and legible. Folded into the running fold-in pass.
- Fold-in #3 completed 2026-11-20 08:15 CST: psychohistory direction (heuristics passage + new named gap: calibration blocked on the corpus, not on method; heuristics remain PROPOSED, direction != ratification), staging practice + corollary (outer/inner sandbox placement, disaster-into-information), and the trial-by-error epistemology (smiley preserved) all recorded in the foundation scroll and SKILL.md. sql.phext 84,051 B sha 082b416c..., --check CURRENT, round-trip byte-exact; SKILL.md synced commit 38b6c037; skills.phext CURRENT sha e430fdef.... Morning teaching state: moves 1-3 recorded, move 4+ still open; hard-stops-vs-warnings still open; diagram syntax + census-mapping gaps still open; MOAT legs still outstanding.
- Hard-stops-vs-warnings question ANSWERED (Will, 2026-11-20 ~12:11 CST, voice call): warn by default. His ruling: the SQL interface is a training module for LLMs — it helps the model be aware of the implications of its decisions; a query structurally designed to delete all the data may be exactly what the user wants (e.g. clearing a table full of test data), so no general rule applies — it is always context-specific. Warnings are parameterized (a tool implementer can bypass them); safe by default, the Git mentality: don't lose user data needlessly. Actuation is tiered by scored intent probability — the probability the user actually intends the change requested (~0% for deleting the whole database): below 30% definitely warn; multiple levels of warning even up to ~70%. Warning levels are preconfigurable by user level: a novice can be warned about every potentially destructive change (any DELETE at all); an expert configures a higher threshold. Folded in the same session: ruling recorded verbatim (will_said) on sql.phext's foundation scroll (33.5.10/7.7.10/5.4.10) and the heuristics material revised PROPOSED -> RULED there and in the sql-legacy skill (proposal history kept, labelled superseded); sql.phext rebuilt, --check CURRENT, round-trip byte-exact.
