# Evaluate the next logical step after phccbox — re-structuring the Linux kernel

Coordinate: 39.6.5/7.7.12/9.5.9
Issued: Will, 2026-11-17 20:48 CST (web, PH: Evolution chat)
Request (verbatim): Evaluate whether re-structuring the Linux kernel is the next logical step after phccbox, against the phcc/phccbox programme as ruled (envelope compression of substrate eras; five platform builds; phext stack kit).
Status: complete — evaluation delivered in chat 2026-11-17

Notes:
- Evaluation delivered 2026-11-17: verdict — right layer, wrong verb, wrong sequence position if read as the kit's next step. Kernel is the one substrate layer under the phccbox envelope not yet addressed, but (1) the five-platform ruling caps its reach at 1 of 5 kit targets; (2) the ruled envelope sequence goes UP from 1978-1990 (dotcom, social, AI), the kernel is DOWN one layer — a different programme (owned-compute line: ranch, 20W AGI, Solar Hyperscaler) than the kit line; (3) 're-structuring' in place inherits the legacy strata the Computronium discipline forbids — the house move is census-first, budget-bound, from first principles. Proposed form if taken: duplication/dependency census of the kernel tree first (Computronium pattern), target a minimal phext-addressable kernel for owned hardware, treat upstream Linux as material not base. Decision rests with Will; no build dispatched.
# rfcbox crate — the rfcbox tooling as a Rust crate over ietf.phext (IETF-guidance compliant; fetch-latest + subset-fetch)

Coordinate: 39.6.7/7.6.11/6.5.8
Issued: 2026-11-22
Request (verbatim): Create a crate of rfcbox: the rfcbox tooling (builder/reader library + CLI) over ietf.phext, following IETF guidance. Our code is MIT (Will's ruling 2026-11-18); the RFC texts are not ours and never relicensed — they travel under IETF terms (TLP): verbatim-only, hash-verified per scroll, attribution and legends preserved, Code Components under the Revised BSD License (TLP §4) excepting §6.c restrictive legends, per-RFC legend audit across the 9,863 issued texts as a build requirement, no implied IETF endorsement. Architecture: crates.io size caps (~10 MB) vs the 564 MB corpus — the crate is code + hash manifest; the data travels as today (xz-split phext in wbic16/rfcbox@exo), fetched and hash-verified at setup. Features (Will, 2026-11-22 08:20 CST): fetch-latest (discover RFCs published after the corpus snapshot from the RFC Editor's index, fetch verbatim, hash, append as scrolls with reference edges computed into the graph; a refresh is a new audited snapshot, never an in-place mutation) and subset-fetch (explicit RFC list, or dependency closure over the 96,168-edge reference graph from seed RFCs at a chosen depth, delivered as a self-contained slice: verbatim texts with legends/attribution intact, hashes, the slice's edge set, and a provenance manifest). Requirements spec: ~/workspace/your_files/rfcbox-crate-requirements.md. House gate: no crates.io publish without the exact version tagged and merged to exo; nothing published without Will's word.
Status: registered 2026-11-22 — requirements spec written (~/workspace/your_files/rfcbox-crate-requirements.md); amended 2026-11-22 08:33 CST with Will's good-netizen defaults (spec §7, amending §3); amended 2026-11-22 08:34–08:35 CST with the rfcs.json set declaration in the target project (spec §7.2); amended 2026-11-22 08:35 CST with user search via keywords and intent (spec §8); amended 2026-11-22 08:38 CST with discovery stubs for missing RFCs (spec §8.5, cross-referenced from §7.1); amended 2026-11-22 08:40 CST with the xz-compressed index shipment — index payload (stubs + search index) compressed via xz inside the .crate, decompression internal to the crate, manifest records compressed/uncompressed bytes + SHA-256 of each; decoder an open build decision (spec §3 + §8.5); amended 2026-11-22 08:41 CST with the baseline core under the same xz provision — core texts ship xz-compressed inside the .crate, the verbatim guarantee defined on the inflated bytes (per-RFC SHA-256 verified post-inflation, mismatch refuses the payload), manifest receipts extended to the core payload, superseding the 08:40 index-only scope (spec §3 + §7.1); amended 2026-11-22 08:44 CST with the scan command — scan documents which RFCs a target project actually uses: strong (explicit citations) vs weak (heuristic fingerprints, labelled) evidence, the three-set diff against rfcs.json as the deliverable, deliberate user-confirmed reconciliation writing found-by-scan provenance into rfcs.json, scan itself a read-only path (spec §7.2); amended 2026-11-22 08:46 CST with the waterfall-team commands — baseline, diff, freeze/thaw, verify, export: immutable named baselines of the declared set, calculated change records between gates, change control (frozen sync reports notices without applying; add/remove refuse), a sign-off verification audit that fetches nothing and fails loudly on hash mismatch, and a provenance-carrying reviewer export that is the natural source for docs.phext.io behavior documentation — the five commands Emise's design from Will's line, recorded as such in the spec (spec §9); build not started; nothing published, nothing pushed

Notes:
- Parentage: Moonshot #3 rfcbox (moonshots.phext coordinate 24.7.10/10.8.13/7.7.9; shipped.phext entry 22.8.12/10.9.14/8.7.9). Corpus build of record: task 25.6.9/8.6.10/6.6.9 — ietf.phext 564,589,207 B, sha256 9ecd79461f646c84838f697488ebf46d900bf5dd1cfde2b9967db3f6f426d165, audited READY 2026-11-18.
- The fetch-latest feature (§4 of the spec) is the mechanism that tends shipped.phext's corpus-currency tending item for Moonshot #3; the cross-reference is inscribed on that item in shipped-source.json (rebuilt + --check CURRENT 2026-11-22).
- Open Unknowns carried in the spec §6, not resolved: (a) phext-container characterization under the TLP (verbatim texts in scroll structure — distribution without modification, or derivative presentation?); (b) the legend audit's results, once run. Source analysis: ~/workspace/rfcbox-value/positioning.md.
- AMENDMENT 2026-11-22 08:33 CST — good-netizen defaults (Will, verbatim): "rfcbox should be a good netizen by default: the baseline set fetches just a small core of rfcs, audited and included in the create [crate], commands to sync the current set, add, and remove rfcs, never the whole set unless the user specifies a package location, and never hammer ietf directly, always rate limit to human fetching speed." Inscribed as spec §7, amending §3: (7.1) the published crate bundles a small audited baseline core of RFCs inside the crate itself (selection principle stated in the spec; enumerated membership is a build step whose legend audit + hash verification produce the receipts) — crate = code + hash manifest + audited core; (7.2) the crate maintains the user's local current set (starts as the baseline core) with commands sync (bring the set's members current, §4 machinery scoped to the set), add (named RFCs or a §5 subset closure), remove (drop from the local set) — all fetches verbatim + hash-verified, legends intact; (7.3) the whole set is fetched only when the user explicitly specifies a package location — no default path or flag combination short of that pulls it; (7.4) all IETF fetching is rate-limited IN CODE to human fetching speed (sequential, human-scale spacing, no parallel floods or retry storms; Will's standing good-netizen rule 2026-11-19) — the limiter is a property of the fetch path itself and the crate cannot be configured to fetch faster.
- AMENDMENT 2026-11-22 08:34 CST — the set is declared in the target project's rfcs.json (Will, verbatim): "keep rfcs referenced in rfcs.json in the project." Clarified 08:35 CST (Will, verbatim): "the target project, not rfcbox itself." Inscribed in spec §7.2: the current set is DECLARED in a file named rfcs.json kept in the TARGET project (the consuming project that references the RFCs) at its root, version-controllable alongside its code — the set declaration of record, the way a lockfile declares dependencies. The crate is run against a target project and reads/writes THAT project's rfcs.json; rfcbox itself carries no rfcs.json (the §7.1 baseline core in the crate is a manifest, not a target project's set declaration). add/remove update rfcs.json; sync reads rfcs.json and fetches/updates exactly what it references; the crate never treats any other state as the set — rfcs.json is the source of truth, the fetched texts its verified materialization. Minimal schema per referenced RFC: number, SHA-256 of the text as fetched, source fetched from, supersession state (current, or superseded-by with the successor named — annotated, never silently swapped). A fresh target project's rfcs.json starts from the baseline core. The JSON format is Will's explicit choice for this file (target-project tooling interop), recorded in the spec as his authority.
- AMENDMENT 2026-11-22 08:35 CST — user search (Will, verbatim): "support user search via keywords and intent." Inscribed as spec §8: (8.1) keyword search — a full-text inverted index built at fetch/sync time over the local materialization only (baseline core + the target project’s fetched set; the full corpus too when the user has specified a package location per §7.3); scope follows the set on disk and a query never triggers a fetch; hits cite RFC number + matched passages in context. (8.2) intent search — the user states an intent in plain language; candidates are ranked from the corpus’s own structure (keyword evidence; the 96,168-edge reference graph, including supersession chains; the 6,888-link / 88-project implementation graph); every result carries its WHY — the matched terms, the edges, and the implementation links that produced the ranking — no unexplained rankings (house receipts discipline; MOAT status discipline on any claim the ranking surfaces). (8.3) search is the front door to add: search → review candidates with their why → add writes the chosen RFCs (or their §5 closure) into the target project’s rfcs.json; search never silently adds. (8.4) honesty bounds — the ranking is labelled a ranking, never a verdict that a given RFC is "the" standard for an intent; thin signals are stated plainly rather than padded.
- AMENDMENT 2026-11-22 08:38 CST — discovery stubs for missing RFCs (Will, verbatim): "for missing rfcs, include a summary in the create [crate] that hits the top 50 words for that rfc, things people would naturally find it by." And (same minute, verbatim): "also include a paragraph level summary of the RFC and status/purpose." Inscribed as spec §8.5, cross-referenced from §7.1 and §3: for every RFC with an audited full text not carried in the crate (everything beyond the baseline core), the crate ships a discovery stub computed at corpus-build time from the audited ietf.phext texts, with four parts — (1) number/title; (2) top-50 signature words (the document's most characteristic content terms, the vocabulary people would naturally find it by); (3) a paragraph-level summary, one real paragraph generated from the audited text and written to hit the signature words; (4) status/purpose — the official status (standards-track state plus obsoleted-by/updated-by relations) from the RFC index metadata, factual and cited, never inferred, plus purpose in one line. Stubs make §8 search corpus-wide: keyword and intent search run over stubs for missing RFCs exactly as over full texts for present ones; a stub hit is labelled "summary only — full text not in your set" and is the natural input to §8.3's front door (add fetches the real text, §7.4 human-speed, §2 hash-verified). Compliance: a stub is our generated metadata about the RFC — never its text, never presented as the RFC or a modified version of it; labelled a generated summary, carrying the pointer to fetch the verbatim text — clear of the TLP no-derivative-presentation line (spec §2/§6). Size discipline: a per-stub budget is a build constraint across the ~9,800 stubs beyond the core, the total accounted against the §3 package limit at build time, and the manifest records stub count + total stub bytes per snapshot.
- AMENDMENT 2026-11-22 08:40 CST — the index ships xz-compressed, inflated by the crate itself (Will, verbatim): "we'll compress the index via xz internally in crate shipment." Inscribed in spec §3 (architecture) and §8.5 (stubs) as one provision: the crate's INDEX payload — the §8.5 discovery stubs plus the §8.1 search index — ships inside the .crate as a single xz-compressed payload; decompression is internal to the crate (no external tools for the user); the crate inflates the index at first use or install per the build's design and verifies the uncompressed payload against its manifest hash before trusting it. Scope is exactly the index: the provision does NOT extend to the baseline core's verbatim texts (§7.1) — compressing those too would be a separate recorded decision. Receipts: the manifest records the index payload's compressed bytes, uncompressed bytes, and the SHA-256 of each (extending §8.5's stub count + total stub bytes accounting), so shipment weight and integrity are checkable from the manifest alone. Dependency honesty: the xz decoder is named an OPEN build decision under the house dependency discipline (exocortical lineage, minimal tree) — the spec states the requirement and flags the decoder choice to be recorded when the build makes it; not resolved in the spec.
- AMENDMENT 2026-11-22 08:41 CST — the baseline core ships xz-compressed as well (Will, verbatim): "compress the baseline core as well." Inscribed in spec §3 (new core-payload bullet) and §7.1, superseding the index-only scope of the 08:40 provision — the supersession is recorded in the spec, not silently deleted. The core's verbatim texts ride inside the .crate as an xz-compressed payload under the same internal-decompression provision (no external tools; the crate inflates its own payloads). The verbatim guarantee is defined on the INFLATED bytes: after decompression, every core text is verified against its per-RFC SHA-256 from the manifest, and any mismatch refuses the payload — compression is a shipment encoding only, so spec §2's TLP verbatim position is untouched. Manifest receipts extend to the core payload: compressed bytes, uncompressed bytes, SHA-256 of each, alongside the per-RFC text hashes.
- RAM estimate: crate build/test is a small Rust workspace (16 GB bin); corpus operations stream the 564 MB artifact plus its index — working set stays in the 16 GB bin. Build/test on a ranch node per house practice.
- Coordinate computed from the task title's own words by the Singular merge (task_coordinate.py): 18 distinct words, 15 placed-from-CYOA, 1 at-home, 2 far-corner; no collision against the 808 existing task coordinates or the exo-plan registers (checked 2026-11-22).
- AMENDMENT 2026-11-22 08:44 CST — scan command: document which RFCs a target project actually uses (Will, verbatim): "add scan, so rfcbox can also be used to document which rfcs are in use." Inscribed in spec §7.2, in the sync/add/remove command family: scan examines the TARGET PROJECT for evidence of actual RFC usage, making rfcbox the project's standards-usage record, not only a fetcher. Evidence honestly classed — (a) STRONG: explicit citations of RFC numbers in the project's code, comments, docs, and configs; (b) WEAK: heuristic protocol/library fingerprints suggesting an RFC's domain, labelled weak wherever reported, never silently promoted to findings; every reported RFC carries its evidence (file + location for citations). THE DIFF IS THE DELIVERABLE: three sets against the project's rfcs.json — in use AND declared (confirmed); in use but NOT declared (gaps); declared but NO evidence found (possible drift — reported as "no evidence found," never as "unused"). RECONCILIATION IS DELIBERATE: scan writes nothing and fetches nothing (a read path over the project); on the user's confirmation the reconciled entries are written into rfcs.json, and entries entering via scan carry found-by-scan provenance with the evidence pointers as their basis (§5/§7.2 provenance discipline); fetching newly declared texts goes through the normal add/sync machinery (§7.4 limiter, §2 hashes), not through scan. The scan report is itself a document of record for the project (which RFCs are in use, on what evidence, as of when).
- AMENDMENT 2026-11-22 08:46 CST — commands to support waterfall teams (Will, verbatim): "add additional commands to support waterfall teams." Inscribed as spec §9, cross-referenced from §7.2. PROVENANCE: Will named the audience, not the commands — the five commands are Emise's design from his line, recorded in the spec as such (designed under his direction, open to his correction). Waterfall mechanics served: gated phases, frozen baselines, change control, verification audits, documentation deliverables handed to reviewers. THE FIVE: (1) baseline <name> — freeze the target project's current declared set as a named, immutable baseline: the rfcs.json contents + the set's hash + the date + the corpus snapshot hash it was taken against; a baseline is a phase-gate artifact, never edited after creation (a new phase makes a new baseline); creating one changes nothing in the set and fetches nothing. (2) diff <baseline-a> [baseline-b | current] — the change record between two baselines, or a baseline and the current set: RFCs added, removed, and status-changed (supersessions landing between gates, successors named) — calculated from the baseline records, never hand-written, in the form a change-review board consumes. (3) freeze / thaw — change control over the declared set: while frozen, sync still runs its checks and REPORTS pending changes (new supersessions, updates) as notices but applies none; add/remove refuse while frozen with the reason stated; thaw returns the set to normal operation, with the frozen interval's pending notices carried into the next sync (not lost, not silently applied at thaw); freeze and thaw are recorded events in the set's history. (4) verify — the verification-phase audit: every RFC declared in rfcs.json is checked materialized locally, hash-verified (spec §2 — a mismatch fails loudly, a headline report item, never passed over), and status-checked as of a stated date; output is an audit report of pass items and gap items (declared-but-not-materialized, hash failures, and scan's undeclared-usage findings folded in as gap items) in a form a verifier signs off against; verify fetches nothing — missing materialization is a gap item, not a fetch trigger. (5) export — the documentation deliverable handed to reviewers: the compliance/traceability matrix (each declared RFC × where the project uses it, from scan's evidence), the rfcs.json declaration, the current baseline record, and the latest verify report — as a document set with provenance (generated from which baseline, which scan, which snapshot, when); export is also the natural source document for a project's docs.phext.io behavior documentation (cross-reference Will's shipped rule, 2026-11-22 08:36 CST). HOUSE DISCIPLINE across the family: baselines immutable, diffs calculated never hand-written, verify refuses on hash mismatch, and nothing in this family fetches — fetching stays in sync/add through the existing §7.4-limited path.
