Notes:
- 2026-11-12 23:10 CST: Will ordered the fleet-wide install directly; the release build from merged exo (3d9aa522, v1.0.2) is being produced as part of the fleet rollout (task at its own coordinate), which satisfies the build leg for the v1.0 line.
- 2026-11-12 ~23:39 CST: PRs #11 (v1.0.2) and #12 (v1.0.3) merged by Will in order; exo head 1b819c2e. The release batch now covers v1.0.1+v1.0.2+v1.0.3. Fleet runs v1.0.2 binaries from the rollout; a fleet update to v1.0.3 awaits Will's direction.
- 2026-11-13 ~00:00 CST: PR #14 (v1.1.0) merged by Will; exo head 2c23dbea. The v1.0-line release batch is complete on exo through v1.1.0. Fleet binaries remain v1.0.2 pending the enablement pass.
- 2026-11-13: PR #15 (v1.2.0) merged; exo head 833e2f34. Batch on exo complete through v1.2.0.
- 2026-11-13: qexo v1.3.0 (PR #16, libphext parser swap) batches into this release task on merge.
- Deployed 2026-11-13/14: the v1.3.0 build (this batch's release) is the fleet build — 19 nodes run it, signer announces it; linux sha256 496610ef17d2e79ecff7507ade5a6358c881f9a9943720fe087ccb12c70035f8, windows-gnu exe 9fcdf257db8651326b38d823226e9b3c48918c5e2e92aa1645faa37fe15ae802 staged in fleet-evolve/stage/ for the external nodes.
- 2026-11-14: qexo v1.4.0 (PR #18, task 31.5.5/6.5.7/5.5.8) batches into this release task on Will's merge — per-user install, nav-tree list, hostnames, qexo learn, qexo inventory.
- 2026-11-14: v1.4.0 (PR #18) merged by Will and published to crates.io at his direction (crate sha256 4c76777a02a98678ef4bcfd00c412faef78e2f890fc2f01c789791c6bc24727d). Fleet deployment via the signed channel: signer (aletheia-core) runs and announces 1.4.0 (manifest 6.6.6/8.1.3/1.1.1); all 18 peers refused at the update post-check (v1.4.0 doctor requires the learn index absent from 1.3.0-born states) and rolled back cleanly to 1.3.0. Peers take 1.4.0 only after a v1.4.1 fix to the post-check/repair path — Will's call.
- 2026-11-14: the batch through PR #18 (v1.4.0) was published (crates.io 1.4.0). The hotfix line followed the same day: v1.4.1 (PR #19, merged 91eacdd8, tagged v1.4.1, crates.io 1.4.1 — published but never rolled out; consumer proof caught a learn regression) and v1.4.2 (PR #20, merged 5b72cf5a, tagged v1.4.2, crates.io 1.4.2), which the fleet now runs (19/19). Release legs for the v1.x line through v1.4.2 are therefore all satisfied; future merges batch here anew.
- v1.5.0 (wizard TUI) merged 2026-11-14 (PR #21, ec2974d2) — batches into this release task; publish awaits Will's direction.
- v1.5.0 LIVE on crates.io 2026-11-14 (tag v1.5.0 on exo 81254808, crate sha256 c9e8dfc8…). Fleet rollout still NOT run — signer still announces 1.4.2, fleet on 1.4.2; the rollout remains a separate act awaiting Will's word.
- 2026-11-14: v1.5.0 FLEET ROLLOUT COMPLETE at Will's direction ('upgrade the fleet', 03:05 CST). Built from the tagged tree (exo 81254808, tag v1.5.0) on aletheia-core — linux sha256 5eb85f754b56c651ba530b15516a30ea59b19c3b32e9330d9f19a6e1ebe4b897, windows exe sha256 600901b131374ecc3cb69fa1cd2452566aa5010222b20a8c5a4018ccbf63a754 staged at fleet-keys/qexo-v1.5.0.exe. Signer swapped first (1.4.2 kept as qexo.prev-1.4.2), then released: manifest 6.6.6/8.1.5/1.1.1, signature 6.6.6/8.1.5/1.1.2, release receipt 6.6.6/2.1.25/1.1.1. ALL 18 leaves took the genuine signed-channel update (updated: 1.4.2 → 1.5.0, on-node sha256 verified, exit 0) — ZERO refusals, no bootstrap swaps: the first channel-clean rollout. 19/19 on v1.5.0; doctor clean on signer + rock-mountain + yeet-max (32 ok, 0 findings); serve active everywhere; yeet-max evolve receipt 6.6.6/2.1.25/1.1.1 (gossip 18 polled / 0 learned / 306 confirmed); timers OFF everywhere (read, never changed). Records: ~/workspace/qexo-task/rollout-v1.5.0/ROLLOUT.md + REGISTRY-v1.5.0.md. The v1.5.0 release leg is fully satisfied (published + deployed).
- 2026-11-14: v1.6.0 merged into quarterexo exo (8f996751d8) + phccbox roster (f8f179f20f). Publish of the 1.6.x line to crates.io and the fleet rollout await Will's word; the learn --app twin (→1.6.1) is in flight, so the publish decision is his: v1.6.0 now from f8f179f20f, or hold for 1.6.1 as the synced release.
- DECISION (Will, 2026-11-14 03:49 CST): hold for 1.6.1 — do NOT publish v1.6.0. Once the learn --app twin merges, publish 1.6.1 as the single synced release for both repos and roll the fleet on the same version, per the plan Will approved in chat.

Results:
- Release outcome for the v1.3.0 batch: PUBLISHED to crates.io 2026-11-13 23:37 CST (registry created_at 2026-11-14T05:37:45Z): qexo v1.3.0 is LIVE — max_version 1.3.0, dependency libphext ^0.6.0 recorded, license MIT, crate checksum 9042f406c4517d036ee15b74480dbd8f1dc9afdfd89fd40dae7777152236b09a (1,483,646 B). Published from aletheia-core from a fresh checkout of merged exo 5574f214e7 (PR #16 merge) in /home/wbic16/qexo-publish-v1.3.0 with a distinct target dir (fleet-evolve trees untouched). Gates: cargo test 52/52, cargo package --list complete, cargo publish --dry-run clean (upload line reached). Consumer proof: fresh-CARGO_HOME `cargo install qexo --version 1.3.0` from the registry built and the binary runs (qexo v1.3.0). Sparse index serves the crate (HTTP 200); the crates.io HTML page lagged (404) minutes after publish while the API already served the record. Preflight: no prior `qexo` crate existed — the name was unclaimed. Manifest note: required metadata present (description, MIT license); repository/keywords/categories absent (optional) — a metadata-polish PR is a possible follow-up, not a blocker. This closes register #53's publish lineage (qexo 0.3.0-era wait, blocked on a crates.io credential).
- v1.6.1 release run end to end 2026-11-14 (Will: 'hold for 1.6.1' 03:49 CST; 'publish and update - send me the apk' 03:56 CST). PUBLISH: tag v1.6.1 created on quarterexo exo 5f092646e7 (PR #24 merge), remote-verified; gates on the tagged tree — status clean before tests and after the cargo run, cargo test 91/91, package 33 files, dry-run clean; cargo publish → crates.io max_version 1.6.1, MIT, dep libphext ^0.6.0, crate sha256 ae8c1533173089773afb72d50e2535d8f425eead485a76c15d874503ac84143a (1,548,736 B), sparse index 200 with matching cksum; consumer proof green (fresh install runs 1.6.1, learn --status 22 cards, learn --app answers scripted status, inventory 12 entries incl. phccbox). FLEET: signer swapped first (1.5.0 kept as qexo.prev-1.5.0) and released via the INTERNAL OpenPGP — manifest 6.6.6/8.1.6/1.1.1, signature 6.6.6/8.1.6/1.1.2, receipt 6.6.6/2.1.26/1.1.1, served sha = build hash ba5ec929…; all 18 leaves took the genuine signed-channel update (GnuPG-verifying 1.5.0 leaves accepted the internally-signed offer — the watch item cleared in production), zero refusals; 19/19 on 1.6.1, serve active, doctor 32 ok / 0 findings (signer + yeet-max), yeet-max evolve receipt 6.6.6/2.1.27/1.1.1, timers OFF read everywhere. Records: ~/workspace/qexo-task/publish-v1.6.1/PUBLISH.md, ~/workspace/qexo-task/rollout-v1.6.1/ROLLOUT.md, REGISTRY-v1.6.1.md. v1.6.0 was never published (held for 1.6.1 per Will).
# Scrollspace traversals — shareable q-link tours (unbounded, zoom steps), plus record / replay / store in your account

Coordinate: 40.7.8/10.7.12/7.7.11
Issued: 2026-11-19 00:50 CST
Request (verbatim): Can you add a mode to scrollspace.html where we can share a traversal list via the query string? On load, the page navigates through a set of waypoints specified by the q variable. / include the ability to zoom in or out after orienting on a node / unbounded traversals, so you can share how you linked two ideas / include the ability to record and replay traversals and store them in your account
Status: closed — traversal mode shipped in two stages (2026-11-19)

Notes:
- Parent lineage: 29.6.11/9.7.14/7.6.9 (Scrollspace UI), 22.7.8/8.6.9/6.6.9 (continuity transitions).
- Scope: scrollspace.html?q=<waypoints> — on load, the page traverses the listed waypoints in order, so a traversal can be shared as a link. Waypoints are phext coordinates (a full address implies the zoom path macro→meso→micro→plane as needed).
- Build points to resolve in the build, not pre-decided: the exact q encoding (URL-safe, compact); traversal rides the existing animated transition system; how the tour ends (rest at the final waypoint) and how a user takes control back mid-tour (any movement input is the natural candidate).
- Refinement (Will, 2026-11-19 00:51 CST, verbatim): "include the ability to zoom in or out after orienting on a node" — the waypoint spec carries zoom steps: after orienting on a node, a traversal can zoom in or out (zoom actions are expressible in the q list, not just arrivals).
- Refinement (Will, 2026-11-19 00:51 CST, verbatim): "unbounded traversals, so you can share how you linked two ideas" — no artificial cap on the waypoint count; the traversal is the shareable record of a link-path between two ideas, however many hops it took. The q encoding must scale accordingly (compact per-waypoint encoding; URL length is a transport fact, not a format limit).
- Refinement (Will, 2026-11-19 00:51 CST, verbatim): "include the ability to record and replay traversals and store them in your account" — record a traversal live as you navigate; replay stored traversals; persistence is server-side in the user’s phext-edit account (not just a local link).
- Staging (one stage at a time): stage A = the stateless q-link mode (share/replay from the URL); stage B = record + account storage + replay from the account (phext-edit backend leg: per-account traversal store + API).
